hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"601","628 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hammad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) specifically the like of it, except that he said: she leaves prayer on the days of her menstruation. So the people of this view placed upon a woman with prolonged bleeding to perform ghusl for every prayer, due to these reports we mentioned. Others opposed them and said: what she must do is perform one ghusl for Zuhr and 'Asr, praying Zuhr at the end of its time and 'Asr at the start of its time, and one ghusl for Maghrib and 'Isha, praying them with it, delaying the first and advancing the second, as she did for Zuhr and 'Asr, and perform ghusl for Fajr."
"602","629 - And they went in that to what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri reported to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Zaynab bint Jahsh, she said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying she had prolonged bleeding, so he said: Let her sit out the days of her cycles, then perform ghusl, delay Zuhr and hasten 'Asr, perform ghusl and pray, delay Maghrib and hasten 'Isha, perform ghusl and pray, and perform ghusl for Fajr."
"603","630 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, that a Muslim woman suffered prolonged bleeding, so they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it except that he said: the length of her days."
"604","631 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that a woman suffered prolonged bleeding in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so she was commanded, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention her leaving prayer on the days of her cycles nor on the days of her menstruation."
"605","632 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Suhayl, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from Asma' bint 'Umays, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Fatimah bint Abi Hubaysh has suffered prolonged bleeding for such-and-such time and has not prayed. He said: Glory be to Allah, this is from Satan; let her sit in a tub, and when she sees yellowness over the water, let her perform one ghusl for Zuhr and 'Asr, then perform one ghusl for Maghrib and 'Isha, and perform ablution in between. His saying: and perform ablution in between may mean ablution for occurrences requiring breaking of purification, or for Fajr. So there is no proof in it against what preceded, from the hadith of Shu'bah and Sufyan. They said: these reports are reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we mentioned, joining Zuhr and 'Asr with one ghusl, joining Maghrib and 'Isha with one ghusl, and singling out Fajr with one ghusl. By this we take, and it is worthier than the first reports commanding ghusl for every prayer; for what indicates this abrogates that has been reported. They mentioned what..."
"606","633 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: She is Sahlah bint Suhayl ibn 'Amr, who suffered prolonged bleeding, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to order her to perform ghusl for every prayer; when that exhausted her, he ordered her to join Zuhr and 'Asr in one ghusl, Maghrib and 'Isha in one ghusl, and perform ghusl for Fajr. They said: that shows this ruling abrogates the ruling in the first reports, for he ordered it after that, so holding by it is worthier than the first reports. They said: that was also reported from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him). They mentioned what..."
"607","634 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Raja', from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A woman with prolonged bleeding came asking him, but he did not rule for her, and said to her: Ask another. She went to Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him, so he said to her: Do not pray as long as you see blood, so she returned to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and told him, so he said, may Allah have mercy on him: He nearly made you disbelieve. Then she asked 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), so he said: That is a prick from Satan, or a wound in the womb; perform ghusl once for every two prayers, and pray. She met Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) after, asked him, and he said: I find for you only what 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said."
"608","635 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Mujahid, he said: It was said to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): Our land is a cold land. He said: She delays Zuhr, hastens 'Asr, performs one ghusl for both, delays Maghrib, hastens 'Isha, performs one ghusl for both, and performs ghusl for Fajr. So these went to these reports. Others opposed them and said: a woman with prolonged bleeding leaves prayer on the days of her cycles, then performs ghusl and ablution for every prayer and prays. They went in that to what..."
"609","636 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus al-Susi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Isa narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Fatimah bint Abi Hubaysh came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I suffer prolonged bleeding and the blood never stops for me, so he ordered her to leave prayer on the days of her cycles, then perform ghusl and ablution for every prayer, and pray, even if blood drips onto the mat in drops."
"610","637 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us. Ha. 638 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Fatimah bint Abi Hubaysh came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I menstruate a month and two months. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is not menstruation; it is only a vein of your blood; when menstruation comes, leave prayer, and when it ends, perform ghusl for your purity; then perform ablution for every prayer."
"611","639 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: I read to Sharik from Abu al-Yaqzan. Ha. 640 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik reported to us, from Abu al-Yaqzan, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A woman with prolonged bleeding leaves prayer on the days of her menstruation; then she performs ghusl and ablution for every prayer, and fasts and prays. They said: the like of that was reported from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him); they mentioned what..."
"612","641 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik reported to us, from Abu al-Yaqzan, from 'Adi ibn Thabit, from his father, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) the like of it, meaning the like of his hadith from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we mentioned in the preceding section. He said: in what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) of this view. An objector opposed them and said: As for the hadith of Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) which he reported from Hisham from 'Urwah, it is a mistake. For the memorizers from Hisham ibn 'Urwah reported it otherwise; they mentioned what..."
"613","642 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, he said: 'Amr, Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, Malik, and al-Layth reported to me, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, that he reported to them from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Fatimah bint Abi Hubaysh came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while she suffered prolonged bleeding, and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I am never pure. Shall I leave prayer forever? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is only a vein, not menstruation; when menstruation comes, leave prayer, and when its measure goes, wash the blood from you then pray."
"614","643 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father and Hisham, both from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) the like of it. Thus the memorizers reported this hadith from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, not as Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) reported. Part of the proof against them is that Hammad ibn Salamah reported this hadith from Hisham... and added a word showing agreement with Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him)."
"615","644 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of the hadith of Yunus from Ibn Wahb, and the hadith of Muhammad ibn 'Ali from Sulayman ibn Dawud, except that he said: When its measure goes, wash the blood from you, perform ablution and pray. In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to perform ablution along with ghusl, and that ablution is the ablution for every prayer; this is the meaning of Abu Hanifah's (may Allah have mercy on him) hadith. And Hammad ibn Salamah in your view regarding Hisham ibn 'Urwah is no less than Malik, al-Layth, and 'Amr ibn al-Harith. So the soundness of the report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a woman with prolonged bleeding performing ablution at the time of every prayer is established. Yet what we mentioned earlier in this chapter was also reported. We wanted to examine that to know what should be acted upon. What was reported at the start of this chapter, that he ordered Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) bint Jahsh to perform ghusl for every prayer, is abrogated, by what we reported in the second section, in the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud from al-Wahbi about Sahlah bint Suhayl: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had ordered her ghusl for every prayer, then when it exhausted her he ordered her to join Zuhr and 'Asr with one ghusl, Maghrib and 'Isha with one ghusl, and perform ghusl for Fajr..."
"616","645 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, al-Mujalid ibn Sa'id, and Bayan narrated to us, they said: We heard 'Amir al-Sha'bi narrating from Qumayr, wife of Masruq, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said about a woman with prolonged bleeding: she leaves prayer on the days of her menstruation, then performs one ghusl, and ablution for every prayer."
"617","646 - Husayn ibn Nasr and 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Firas and Bayan, from al-Sha'bi... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain. When what we mentioned from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) was reported, of her ruling after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the rulings of a woman with prolonged bleeding were reported - ghusl for every prayer, joining two prayers with one ghusl, and leaving prayer on cycle days then ghusl and ablution for every prayer, all reported from her - her answer establishes that ruling as abrogating the other two; for in our view she may not abandon the abrogating and rule by the abrogated, else her report would fall. When this is established as abrogating, it must be held, with no opposition allowed. This is one possible meaning of these reports. Another is possible: what was reported about Fatimah bint Abi Hubaysh does not contradict Sahlah bint Suhayl; for Fatimah's days were known, while Sahlah's were unknown except that her blood stops at times and returns at times, and she knew she had not exited menstruation after her ghusl until she prayed both prayers together. If so, we hold both hadiths, each on its application. As for Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her), it was reported variously. Some mention from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her)..."
"618","22 - Chapter on the ruling of urine of animals whose meat is eaten. 647 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, he said: People of 'Uraynah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah and found it unhealthy. He said: If you went out to a herd of ours and drank of their milk. Qatadah mentioned that he remembered from him: their urine."
"619","648 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, Qatadah, and Humayd, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and he said: of their milk and their urine. So a group held that urine of what is eaten is pure, its ruling like its meat. Among them Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made it a remedy for their ailment, it is lawful; had it been unlawful he would not have treated them with it, for it is a disease not a cure, as he said in the hadith of 'Alqamah ibn Wa'il ibn Hujr."
"620","649 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. Ha. 650 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Alqamah ibn Wa'il, from Tariq ibn Suwayd al-Hadrami, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, in our land are grapes we press and drink from. He said: No. I repeated, and he said: No. I said: O Messenger of Allah, we seek cure with it for the sick. He said: That is a disease, not a cure. And as 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said."
"621","651 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: 'Abdullah said: Allah would not place cure in filth or in what He forbade."
"622","652 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Abu Wa'il, he said: A man of us complained and intoxicant was described for him, so we came to 'Abdullah and asked him, and he said: Allah did not place your cure in what He forbade you."
"623","653 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from 'Ata', he said: 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: O Allah, do not heal one who seeks cure by wine. They said: When it is established by these reports that cure is not in what was forbidden, it is established by the first report, in which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made camel urine a remedy, that it is pure, not unlawful. And there was also reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what..."
"624","654 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Hubayrah narrated to us, from Hanash ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the urine and milk of camels is cure for looseness of their bellies. They said: in that is confirmation of what we described. Others opposed them and said: Camel urine is impure, its ruling like their blood, not like their milk and meat. They said: As for what you reported in the hadith of the 'Uraniyyin, that was only for necessity, with no proof it is permitted without necessity, for we have seen things permitted in necessities but not otherwise, and reports about them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were reported."
"625","655 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Hammam reported to me. Ha. 656 - And 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah reported to me, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that al-Zubayr and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of lice, so he permitted them silk shirts in a campaign of theirs. Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I saw on each of them a silk shirt. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted silk to those men for the itch they had, as treatment, with no proof from permitting it for that cause that it is permitted without that cause. Likewise what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the 'Uraniyyin for their ailments is no proof it is permitted without those ailments. The prohibition of silk does not deny it is lawful in necessity, nor that it treats some ailments. Likewise the sanctity of urine without necessity is no proof it is unlawful in necessity. So the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying about wine, that it is a disease not a cure, is because they sought cure by it as wine, which is unlawful. Likewise 'Abdullah's saying, in our view - that Allah did not place your cure in what He forbade you - is about what they did with wine, magnifying it, counting it a cure in itself. So he said to them: Allah did not place your cure in what He forbade you..."
"626","657 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Jabir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ali, he said: No harm in the urine of camels, cattle, and sheep to be treated with. It is possible he went to that because in his view they are lawful and pure in all states, as Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said. And it is possible he permitted treatment with them for necessity, not because they are pure in themselves nor permitted without necessity."
"627","658 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to seek cure with camel urine, seeing no harm in it. This too bears what the saying of Muhammad ibn 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) bore."
"628","659 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Karim, from 'Ata', he said: Of whatever is eaten, there is no harm in its urine. This is a hadith clear in meaning."
"629","660 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that he disliked the urine of camels, cattle, and sheep, or words to this effect."
"630","23 - Chapter on the description of tayammum, how it is. 661 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abdullah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from 'Ammar, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the verse of tayammum was revealed, so we struck one strike for the face, then struck a strike for the hands to the shoulders, outer and inner."
"631","662 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, they said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"632","663 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah reported to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, that he reported to him, from his father, from 'Ammar, he said: We wiped ourselves with dust along with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we wiped our faces and our hands to the shoulders."
"633","664 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, that Ibn Shihab narrated to him that 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah reported to him from his father, from 'Ammar the like of it."
"634","665 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubaydullah, from his father, from 'Ammar, he said: We performed tayammum with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the shoulders."
"635","666 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b reported to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; a necklace of 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) perished, so they sought it until morning, with no water among the people; then the concession of tayammum with clean earth was revealed; so the Muslims stood, struck their hands to the earth, wiped their faces with them and the outer hands to the shoulders, and the inner to the armpits."
"636","667 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), from 'Ammar ibn Yasir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this and said: thus is tayammum, a strike for the face, and a strike for the arms to the shoulders and armpits. Others opposed them and split into two groups. One group said: tayammum is for the face and hands to the elbows. And one group said: tayammum is for the face and palms. Part of the proof of these two groups against the first is that 'Ammar ibn Yasir did not mention that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to perform tayammum thus; he only reported their act. It is possible that when the verse was revealed it was not revealed complete, but only: perform tayammum with clean earth was revealed, without explaining how; that in their view covered whatever tayammum they did, with no fixed time nor specified limb, until there was revealed after: wipe your faces and hands with it. And among what proves what we said is what..."
"637","668 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abu al-Aswad who narrated to him that he heard 'Urwah reporting to him from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from a campaign until at al-Mu'arras near Madinah, I dozed in the night, and upon me was a necklace called al-simt, reaching the navel, and as I dozed it came off my neck. When I stopped with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Fajr prayer, I said: O Messenger of Allah, my necklace fell from my neck. He said: O people, your mother has lost her necklace, so seek it. The people sought it with no water with them, and busied themselves seeking it until prayer came, and they found the necklace, unable to find water. Some performed tayammum to the palms, some to the shoulder, and some over the body. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the verse of tayammum was revealed. In this hadith the revelation of the tayammum verse was after they performed this differing tayammum, part to the shoulders; so we knew their tayammum was done only with a prior basis of tayammum, and by her saying: Allah revealed the tayammum verse, we knew what was revealed after their act is the description of tayammum. This is the sense of 'Ammar's hadith in our view. And also proving that this verse negates what they did is that 'Ammar ibn Yasir who reported that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported otherwise from him about the tayammum he did after that, contrary to that. Among it is what..."
"638","669 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from 'Azrah, from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, that 'Ammar ibn Yasir asked the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about tayammum, so he ordered him the face and palms."
"639","670 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Dharr ibn 'Abdullah narrating, from Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, that a man came to 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: I was on a journey and became junub, and found no water. 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do not pray. 'Ammar said: O Commander of the Believers, do you not remember that I and you were in a detachment, became junub and found no water; as for you, you did not pray, and as for me, I rolled in dust. We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him, so he said: As for you, it would have sufficed you; and he said with his hands, striking with them, blowing into them, and wiping his face and palms with them. So 'Ammar, when he rolled, meant tayammum by it, and if that was after the verse, it was in our view, and Allah knows best, because he acted on tayammum for janabah being other than for minor impurity, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him they are the same."
"640","671 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Za'idah and Shu'bah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Malik, from 'Ammar that he said: (to the joint) and he did not raise it."
"641","672 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from 'Ammar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: It suffices you to say thus - and al-A'mash struck the earth with his hands, then blew them and wiped his face and palms with them."
"642","673 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam reported to me, from Dharr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, from 'Ammar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: It would have sufficed you thus - and Shu'bah struck with his palms to the earth and brought them near his mouth; he blew into them, then wiped his face and palms. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah said in this hadith's chain, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, but it is from Dharr, from Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father."
"643","674 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah, he said: I heard Dharr narrating from Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father the like of it. Salamah said: I do not know, did it reach the forearms or not."
"644","675 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan reported to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Malik, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza the like of it. And he added: So he wiped his face with them and his hands to the halves of the forearms."
"645","676 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain. So 'Ammar's hadith has become confused for us, except that all of them denied it reaching the shoulders and armpits. Thereby what was reported from him in the hadith of 'Ubaydullah from his father, or Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), is negated, and one of the other two views is established. We looked, and Abu Juhaym reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he made tayammum of his face and palms. That is proof for tayammum to the palms. And Nafi' reported from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he performed tayammum to his elbows. I mentioned both hadiths in the chapter on reciting Qur'an for a menstruating woman. And..."
"646","677 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from al-Rabi' ibn Badr, he said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Asla' al-Tamimi, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, so he said to me: O Asla', stand and ready for us. I said: O Messenger of Allah, janabah befell me after you. He was silent to me until Jibril came to him with the verse of tayammum, so he said to me: O Asla', stand and perform tayammum with clean earth, two strikes, a strike for your face and a strike for your arms, their outer and inner. When we reached water, he said: O Asla', stand and perform ghusl. When they differed about tayammum how it is, and these reports differed in it, we returned to reasoning to extract a sound view from these sayings. We considered that, and found ablution upon the limbs Allah mentioned in His Book, while tayammum was dropped from some; dropped from the head and feet, so tayammum is upon part of what ablution is upon. Thereby the view that it is to the shoulders is void, for since it is void from the head and feet - which are washed - it is likelier not to be obligatory on what is not washed. Then they differed about the forearms, are they wiped or not? We saw the face wiped with earth as washed with water, and saw the head and feet with nothing wiped of them. So what tayammum fell from in part fell from in whole, and what tayammum was obligatory in was like ablution equally; for it was made a substitute for it. When it is established that part of what is washed of the hands when water exists is wiped when water is absent, it is established that tayammum of the hands is to the elbows by analogy and reasoning on what we explained..."
"647","678 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, and from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Nafi', he said: I asked Ibn 'Umar about tayammum. So he struck the earth with his hands and wiped his hands and face with them, and struck another strike, and wiped his forearms with them."
"648","679 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Kunasi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) the like of it."
"649","680 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) the like of it."
"650","681 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', that 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) came from al-Jurf until at al-Mirbad, performed tayammum with clean earth, wiped his face and hands to the elbows, then prayed."
"651","682 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: 'Azrah ibn Thabit narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A man came to him and said: Janabah befell me, and I rolled in dust. He said: Did you become a donkey... and he struck the earth with his hands, wiped his face, then struck the earth with his hands, wiped his hands to the elbows with them, and said: Thus is tayammum. And the like of that was also reported from al-Hasan."
"652","683 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan that he said: A strike for the face and palms, and a strike for the forearms to the elbows."
"653","684 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from al-Hasan the like of it, and he did not say to the elbows."
"654","24 - Chapter on ghusl of Friday. 685 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Muhriz narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Tawus, he said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): They mentioned that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ghusl on Friday, wash your heads even if not junub, and take of perfume. Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: As for ghusl, yes; as for perfume, I do not know it."
"655","686 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah reported to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Tawus said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both)... then he mentioned the like of it."
"656","687 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) the like of it."
"657","688 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Waththab, he said: I heard a man ask Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) about ghusl on Friday, so he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us with it."
"658","689 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Nafi', and from Yahya ibn Waththab, they said: We heard Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that."
"659","690 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, that he heard Nafi' narrating, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"660","691 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from the hadith of Salim ibn 'Abdullah, from the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"661","692 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"662","693 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"663","694 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"664","695 - 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud Abu Bishr al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"665","696 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar on the pulpit saying: Did you not hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When one of you comes to Friday, let him perform ghusl?"
"666","697 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas, from Bukayr ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Nafi', freedman of 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Upon every adult is going early to Friday, and upon whoever goes early to the mosque is ghusl."
"667","698 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah, Yazid ibn Mawhab, and 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbad al-Basri narrated to us, they said: al-Mufaddal narrated to us... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"668","699 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Shaybah, from Talq ibn Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command ghusl on Friday."
"669","700 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Ansar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a right upon every Muslim to perform ghusl on Friday, and to perfume himself from perfume if he has any."
"670","701 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind. Ha. 702 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Dawud, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Ghusl is obligatory upon every Muslim once every week, and it is Friday."
"671","703 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Ghusl on Friday is obligatory upon every adult."
"672","704 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Safwan... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"673","705 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad reported to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Part of the right upon every Muslim is to perform ghusl on Friday, and to touch perfume if his family has any; if they have no perfume, then water is perfume. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to making ghusl obligatory on Friday, arguing with these reports. Others opposed them and said: Ghusl on Friday is not obligatory, but it is among what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded, for reasons that existed. Among them: what was reported from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) in that:"
"674","706 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi reported to us. Ha. 707 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to me, from 'Ikrimah, he said: Ibn 'Abbas was asked about ghusl on Friday, is it obligatory? He said: No, but it is purification and good; whoever performs ghusl, it is good, and whoever does not, it is not obligatory upon him. And I shall tell you how it began: people were strained, wearing wool, working on their backs, and the mosque was narrow with a low roof, only an arbor. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out on a hot day, and people had sweated in that wool until odors rose, harming one another. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found that odor and said: O people, when this day comes, perform ghusl, and let each touch the best of his oil and perfume. Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Then Allah brought good, and they wore other than wool, were spared work, and their mosque was widened. So this is Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) reporting that the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for ghusl was not as obligation, but for a cause, then that cause went and ghusl went; and he is one from whom it was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he commanded ghusl. And something was reported from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) in that."
"675","708 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn 'Iyad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id. Ha. 709 - And Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Yahya, he said: I asked 'Amrah about ghusl on Friday, and she mentioned that she heard 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: People were workers for themselves, going in their states, so he said: If only you performed ghusl. So this is 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) reporting that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only urged them to ghusl for the cause Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) reported, and did not make it binding upon them; and she is one from whom we reported in the first section that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command ghusl on that day. And what indicates that was not taken as obligation with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab was reported."
"676","710 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan reported to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn 'Abbas: that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), while he was delivering the sermon on Friday, a man came and entered the mosque, so 'Umar said to him: Now when you performed ablution. He said: I did no more, when I heard the call, than perform ablution, then come. When the Commander of the Believers entered I reminded him, so I said: O Commander of the Believers, I heard what he said. He said: And what did he say? I said: He said: I did no more than perform ablution when I heard the call, then come. He said: Yet he knew that we were commanded with other than that. I said: What is it? He said: Ghusl. I said: Are you - O first Muhajirun - or all the people? He said: I do not know."
"677","711 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah, he said: A man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque on Friday, while 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was delivering the sermon. 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What hour is this? He said: O Commander of the Believers, I returned from the market and heard the call, and did no more than perform ablution. 'Umar said: Ablution too? Though you knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command ghusl? Malik said: And the man is 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"678","712 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father the like of it. Except that he did not mention Malik's saying that he is 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"679","713 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar the like of it."
"680","714 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). Ha."
"681","715 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: While 'Umar was delivering the sermon to the people, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan entered, so 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) reproached him, saying: What is with men who delay after the call... then he mentioned the like of it."
"682","716 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man of the first Muhajirun entered the mosque while 'Umar was delivering the sermon, so 'Umar called to him: What hour is this? He said: It was only ablution then coming, so 'Umar said: And ablution too? Though you knew that we were commanded with ghusl? Abu Ja'far said: In these reports are more than one meaning negating the obligation of ghusl. One: 'Uthman did not perform ghusl and sufficed with ablution. And 'Umar said: You knew that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us ghusl. And 'Umar did not order him to return for the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command of ghusl. In that is proof that the ghusl he commanded was not in their view as obligation, but for the cause Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) and 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) stated, or otherwise. Had it not been so, 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) would not have left it, nor would 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) have been silent about ordering him to return to perform ghusl, in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who heard that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as 'Umar heard, and knew the meaning he intended, denying nothing of that, nor ordering otherwise. In this is consensus from them negating the obligation of ghusl. And there was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that was by way of choice and attaining merit."
"683","717 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi' ibn Subayh narrated to us, from al-Hasan, and from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and excellent; and whoever performs ghusl, then ghusl is good."
"684","718 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us. Ha. 719 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Except that he said: And whoever performs ghusl, then ghusl is better."
"685","720 - Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi' ibn Subayh and Sufyan al-Thawri reported to me, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"686","721 - Ahmad ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-'Attar narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' reported to me, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"687","722 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Khali al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Hamrah al-Amluki narrated to me, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and excellent, and he has fulfilled the obligation; and whoever performs ghusl, then ghusl is better. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified in this hadith that the obligation is ablution, and ghusl is better, for the merit attained by it, not as obligation. If one argues for its obligation by what was reported from 'Ali, Sa'd, Abu Qatadah, and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"688","723 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, he said: I was sitting with Sa'd, and he mentioned ghusl on Friday. His son said: So I did not perform ghusl. Sa'd said: I never thought a Muslim would leave ghusl on Friday."
"689","724 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Murrah reported to me, from Zad han, he said: I asked 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) about ghusl, so he said: Perform ghusl when you wish. I said: I ask you only about the ghusl which is ghusl. He said: Friday, the day of 'Arafah, the day of Fitr, and the day of Adha."
"690","725 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Umar, and from Tawus, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The right of Allah is binding upon every Muslim every seven days, to perform ghusl, wash everything of it, and touch perfume if his family has any."
"691","726 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Mus'ab ibn Thabit narrated to him, that Thabit ibn Abi Qatadah narrated to him, that Abu Qatadah said to him: Perform ghusl for Friday, so he said to him: I have already performed ghusl for janabah."
"692","727 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, that his father would renew ablution after performing ghusl on Friday, so he would perform ablution and not repeat ghusl. It is said to him: As for what was reported from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), there is no proof in it for obligation; for when Zad han said to him: I ask you only about the ghusl which is ghusl, meaning in which attaining it is merit, he said: Friday, the day of Fitr, the day of Sacrifice, and the day of 'Arafah, so he joined part of that to part. Since what he mentioned along with Friday ghusl is not as obligation, so is Friday ghusl. As for what was reported from Sa'd, his saying: I never thought a Muslim would leave ghusl on Friday, it is for the great merit in it with lightness of burden. As for what was reported from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), his saying: The right of Allah is binding upon every Muslim to perform ghusl every seven days, he joined it to his saying: and let him touch perfume if his family has any, so touching perfume was not as obligation, so is ghusl. He heard 'Umar say to 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) what we mentioned, and did not order him to return in his presence, nor deny him that, so that too is proof it is likewise in his view. As for what was reported from Abu Qatadah, of what we mentioned from him in that, it is his intending ghusl for Friday, to attain merit in that; and we have reported from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza contrary to that. And all we clarified in this chapter is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"693","25 - Chapter on istijmar. 728 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb reported to us, that Malik narrated to him. Ha. 729 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Malik, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs istijmar, let him make it odd."
"694","730 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"695","731 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from 'A'idhullah, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say the like of it."
"696","732 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Idris, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"697","733 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to me, from al-Qa'qa', from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to order us, when one of us goes to relieve himself, to use three stones."
"698","734 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to me, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Abu Hazim, from Muslim ibn Qurt, that he heard 'Urwah say: 'A'ishah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you goes out to relieve himself, let him take three stones to clean himself with, for they will suffice him."
"699","735 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur. Ha. 736 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I read to Mansur. Ha. 737 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Salamah ibn Qays, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever performs istijmar, let him make it odd."
"700","738 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn 'Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us. Ha. 739 - And 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn 'Ajlan, he said: al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to order us to use three stones, meaning in istijmar."
"701","740 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from 'Amr ibn Khuzaymah, from 'Umarah ibn Khuzaymah, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about istijmar with three stones among which is no dung."
"702","741 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Jandal ibn Waliq narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Salman, he said: We were forbidden to suffice with fewer than three stones. So a group held that istijmar does not suffice with fewer than three stones, arguing with what we mentioned of these reports. Others opposed them and said: Whatever of them he uses for istijmar and removes filth with, three or more or fewer, odd or not odd, that is his purification. Part of their proof is that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command of oddness here may be as recommendation for oddness, not that what is not odd does not purify. And it may have meant a fixed measure below which does not purify. So we examined that, do we find in it what proves something of that?"
"703","742 - Then Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to me, he said: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Husayn al-Hubrani, from Abu Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever applies kohl, let him make it odd; whoever does, he has done well, and whoever does not, no harm; and whoever performs istijmar, let him make it odd; whoever does, he has done well; and whoever picks his teeth, let him spit; and whoever licks with his tongue, let him swallow; whoever does this has done well, and whoever does not, no harm; and whoever goes to relieve himself, let him screen himself, and if he finds only a dune to gather, let him screen himself with it; for Satan toys with the seats of the children of Adam."
"704","743 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, he said: Husayn al-Himyari narrated to us, he said: Abu Sa'd al-Khayr narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. And he added: Whoever performs istijmar, let him make it odd; whoever does, he has done well, and whoever does not, no harm. So that shows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only commanded oddness in the first reports as recommendation for oddness, not as an obligation without which does not suffice. And there was reported from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what also clarified that."
"705","744 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Zuhayr, he said: Abu Ishaq reported to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he went to relieve himself and said: Bring me three stones. I sought, and found only two stones and dung, so he cast the dung and took the two stones, and said: It is filth."
"706","745 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn 'Abbad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: Ibn Mas'ud said, and he mentioned the like of it. In this hadith is proof that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat to relieve himself in a place with no stones, for his saying to 'Abdullah: Hand me three stones. Had there been any of that in his presence he would not have needed to be handed from elsewhere. When 'Abdullah brought him two stones and dung, and he cast the dung and took the two stones, that shows his use of the two stones, and that he held istijmar with them suffices as istijmar with three suffices. For had istijmar with fewer than three not sufficed, he would not have sufficed with the two stones but would have ordered 'Abdullah to seek a third. In his leaving that is proof of his sufficing with the two stones - this is the sense of this chapter by verifying the meanings of the reports. As for by reasoning, we saw excrement and urine, when washed once with water so their trace or smell goes until nothing remains, that their place is purified. Had their color and smell not gone with that, a second washing is needed. If washed a second time and their color and smell go, it is purified thereby, as purified by one. Had their color and smell not gone with two washings, it must be washed after until their color and smell go. So what is sought in washing them is their removal by whatever washing removes them, with no known measure of washing below which does not suffice. By analogy istijmar with stones is likewise: no known measure of stones is sought such that istijmar with fewer does not suffice, but what removes impurity suffices, little or much. This is the reasoning, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"707","26 - Chapter on istijmar with bones. 746 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid reported to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu 'Uthman ibn Sannah al-Khuza'i, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade anyone to clean himself with a bone or dung."
"708","747 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Jandal ibn Waliq narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Salman, he said: We were forbidden to clean ourselves with a bone or dung."
"709","748 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to me, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith reported to me, from Musa ibn Abi Ishaq al-Ansari, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade anyone to clean himself with a bone, dung, or skin."
"710","749 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan. Ha. 750 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Safwan narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us. Ha. 751 - And 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Qa'qa', from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade cleaning with dung or decayed bones, and decayed bones are bones."
"711","752 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh reported to me, from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas, that Shuyaym ibn Baytan reported to him that he heard Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit al-Ansari that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit, life may be long for you, so tell people that whoever cleans himself with the dung of an animal or a bone, Muhammad is free of him. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that bones are not used for cleaning, and placed one who cleans with them in the ruling of one who did not clean. They argued with these reports. Others opposed them and said: He did not forbid cleaning with bone because cleaning with it is unlike cleaning with stone and other; but he forbade that because it was made provision for the jinn, so the children of Adam were ordered not to soil it upon them. And what clarified that is what..."
"712","753 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not clean yourselves with bone or dung; for they are the provisions of your brothers the jinn."
"713","754 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud that he said: The jinn asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the last night he met them in a Makkan ravine for provision. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every bone falling into your hands, over which Allah's name was mentioned, is at its fullest with meat, and dung is fodder for your animals. They said: The children of Adam soil it upon us. Thereupon he said: Do not clean yourselves with the dung of an animal or a bone; it is the provision of your brothers among the jinn."
"714","755 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Azraqi narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he went out for his need, and he would not turn, so I drew near him, made myself known and cleared my throat. He said: Who is this? I said: Abu Hurayrah. He said: O Abu Hurayrah, seek for me stones to clean myself with, and do not bring me a bone or dung. He said: So I brought him stones carrying them in my garment, placed them beside him, then turned away from him. When he finished his need I followed him and asked him about the stones, bone, and dung, so he said: A delegation of Nasibin of the jinn came to me - and excellent jinn they are - and asked me for provision, so I prayed to Allah for them that they pass by no bone or dung but find food upon it."
"715","756 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain. So it is established by these reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade cleaning with bones for the sake of the jinn, not because they do not purify as stone purifies. And all we went to, that cleaning with bones purifies, is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"716","27 - Chapter on one junub wanting to sleep, eat, drink, or have intercourse. 757 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us. Ha. And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would sleep while junub without touching water."
"717","758 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from the mosque, he prayed what Allah willed, then inclined to his bed and his family; if he had a need he fulfilled it, then he would sleep as he is, without touching water."
"718","759 - Malik ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sayf narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would become junub, then sleep, touching no water, until he would rise after that and perform ghusl."
"719","760 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"720","761 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym reported to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid reported to me, from Abu Ishaq... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"721","762 - Salih narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain. So a group went to this, among them Abu Yusuf, and said: We see no harm in a junub sleeping without performing ablution; for ablution does not take him from janabah to purity. Others opposed them and said: He should perform ablution for prayer before sleeping, and they said: This hadith is a mistake; for it is an abridged hadith, which Abu Ishaq abridged from a long hadith, erring in his abridgment."
"722","763 - And that is that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I came to al-Aswad ibn Yazid - and he was a brother and friend to me. I said: O Abu 'Amr, narrate to me what 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), Mother of the Believers, narrated to you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sleep the first of the night and enliven its end, then if he had a need he fulfilled his need, then he would sleep before touching water; when at the first call, he leapt - and she did not say: stood - and poured water over himself, and she did not say: performed ghusl, and I know what you mean; and if junub, he would perform ablution as a man for prayer. So this al-Aswad ibn Yazid clarified in his hadith, when we mentioned it at length, that when he wanted to sleep while junub he would perform his ablution for prayer. As for her saying: if he had a need he fulfilled it, then he would sleep before touching water, it may be construed upon the water of ghusl, not ablution. And other than Abu Ishaq clarified that, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform his ablution for prayer."
"723","764 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to sleep or eat while junub, he would perform ablution."
"724","765 - Then he reported from al-Aswad, of his own view, the like of that. Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: al-Aswad said: When a man becomes junub and wants to sleep, let him perform ablution. So it is impossible, in our view, that 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would sleep without touching water, then order them after that with ablution; but the hadith in that is what Ibrahim reported. And other than al-Aswad reported from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) what agrees with that also."
"725","766 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb reported to us, he said: Yunus and al-Layth reported to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to sleep, while junub, he would perform his ablution for prayer."
"726","767 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"727","768 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ... then he mentioned it with its chain, the like thereof."
"728","769 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"729","770 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof, with the addition: and he should wash his private part."
"730","771 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, that Abu Amr, the freed slave of Aishah, informed him, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of the hadith of al-Zuhri from Abu Salamah. So this narrator, other than al-Aswad, has narrated from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what agrees with what Ibrahim narrated from al-Aswad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has been narrated from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), as her own statement, the like of that."
"731","772 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she used to say: When one of you has intercourse with his wife and then wants to sleep, let him not sleep until he performs ablution, his ablution for prayer."
"732","773 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Hisham informed us, he said: my father informed me, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the like thereof, with the addition: for he does not know; perhaps his soul may be taken in his sleep. So it is impossible that she had from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything contrary to this and then give a ruling with this. So by what we have mentioned, the invalidity of what was narrated from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, of what we mentioned, is established, and what Ibrahim narrated from al-Aswad is established. It is also possible that what Abu Ishaq meant by his saying: and he would not touch water, is ghusl; for Abu Hanifah has narrated from him something of this nature."
"733","774 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Faddalah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to have intercourse, then repeat it without performing ablution, and sleep without bathing. So what was mentioned that he would not do when he had intercourse before sleeping was ghusl; that does not negate wudu. And there has been narrated from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of that."
"734","775 - Ali ibn Zayd al-Faraidi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, may one of us sleep while he is in a state of major impurity? He said: Yes, and he should perform ablution."
"735","776 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof, with the addition: his ablution for prayer."
"736","777 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sufyan al-Jahdari narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"737","778 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof, with the addition: and wash your private part."
"738","779 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us. (H). 780 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us. (H). 781 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, then all of them agreed and said: from Sufyan, from Abdillah ibn Dinar ... then he mentioned it with its chain, the like thereof."
"739","782 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdillah ibn Dinar ... then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain. And it was narrated from Ammar ibn Yasir and Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of that as well."
"740","783 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave concession to the one in a state of major impurity, when he wants to sleep or drink or eat, to perform ablution, his ablution for prayer."
"741","784 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah and Yahya ibn Ayyub and Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, in a similar manner, from Ibn al-Had, from Abdillah ibn Khabbab, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have been with my wife and I want to sleep. He said: Perform ablution and sleep. So the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the one in major impurity when he wants to sleep have reached the level of succession, with what we have mentioned. And a group of the Companions after him held this view, among them Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her); we have already mentioned that from her as her opinion above. And that has also been narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit."
"742","785 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Luhayah informed me, from Ibn Hubayrah, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: When the one in major impurity performs ablution before sleeping, he has spent the night in a state of purity. So this Zayd ibn Thabit informs that when he performs ablution before sleeping, then sleeps, he is like one who bathed before sleeping, in the reward recorded for one who spends the night pure. And we have mentioned the hadith of al-Hakam from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he wanted to eat while in major impurity, would perform ablution, and from Abu Said al-Khudri what agrees with that. So a group went to this and said: The one in major impurity should not eat until he performs ablution. Others disagreed with them on that and said: There is no harm in eating even if he does not perform ablution."
"743","786 - And they had as proof for that that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Suhaym al-Harrani informed me, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid al-Ayli narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he wanted to eat while in major impurity, would wash his hands. So there has been narrated from Aishah what we mentioned, and there has also been narrated from her the opposite of that, of what we narrated from her that he would perform ablution, his ablution for prayer. Since those conflicted, it is possible in our view - and Allah knows best - that his ablution when he would perform ablution was in the period we mentioned in another chapter than this, that when he had relieved himself he would not speak, so he would perform ablution in order to speak, then mention the name of Allah and eat; then that was abrogated, so he washed his hands for cleanliness and left ablution. Likewise his ablution (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at sleep: it is possible he would do it also to sleep upon remembrance, then that was abrogated, so remembrance of Allah was permitted for the one in major impurity, and the reason for which he performed ablution was lifted. And we have narrated in more than one place from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out of the lavatory and it was said to him: Will you not perform ablution? He said: Do I want to pray, that I should perform ablution? So he informed that he does not perform ablution except for prayer. So in that too is a negation of ablution for the one in major impurity when he wants to sleep or eat or drink. And among what also indicates the abrogation of that is that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated what we mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his answer to Umar. Then there came from him after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what..."
"744","787 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When a man becomes impure and wants to eat or drink or sleep, he washes his hands, rinses his mouth and nose, washes his face and forearms, washes his private part, and does not wash his feet. So this is an incomplete ablution. And it is known that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in that a complete ablution, so this can only be after the abrogation of that from him has been established. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man who has intercourse with his wife then wants to repeat it what..."
"745","788 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes to his wife then wants to repeat, let him perform ablution."
"746","789 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Asim, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain. So it is possible that he commanded this at a time when the one in major impurity could not mention Allah until he performed ablution, so he commanded ablution so that he would mention the name of Allah at his intercourse, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in other than this hadith; then he gave them concession to speak with the remembrance of Allah while in major impurity, so that was lifted. And there has been narrated from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have intercourse then repeat without performing ablution; we have mentioned that in another chapter than this. So this, in our view, abrogates that. If one says: It has been narrated from him that he would go around his wives, and would bathe whenever he had intercourse with one of them, and he mentioned about that what..."
"747","790 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (H). 791 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Rafi, from his aunt Salma, from Abu Rafi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he would go around his wives in a day, would bathe with this one and with this one. So it was said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you made it one bath? He said: This is purer and cleaner and better smelling. It was said to him: In this is what indicates that that was not obligatory, due to his saying: This is purer and better and cleaner. And there has been narrated from him that he went around his wives with one bath."
"748","792 - Yunus and Bahr said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us. (H). 793 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went around his wives with one bath."
"749","794 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mamar, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"750","795 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"751","796 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"752","797 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (H). 798 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"753","799 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Shubah, from Hisham ibn Zayd, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"754","2 - The Book of Prayer. 1 - Chapter of the adhan, how is it? 800 - Ali ibn Mabad and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us. (H). 801-802 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, they said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Saib informed me, he said: Abu Asim said in his hadith: My father and Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah informed me, meaning from Abu Mahdhurah. Rawh said in his hadith: from Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, from Abu Mahdhurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me the adhan as you call it now: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah; I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah; I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, come to prayer; come to success, come to success; Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; there is no god but Allah. 803 - And Rawh said in his hadith: Uthman informed me of all this report from Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, that she heard that from Abu Mahdhurah. 804 - And Abu Asim said in his hadith: Uthman ibn al-Saib informed me of all this report, from his father and from Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, that they heard that from Abu Mahdhurah."
"755","805 - Ali ibn Shaybah and Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, they said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah informed me that Abdillah ibn Muhayriz narrated to him - and he was an orphan in the care of Abu Mahdhurah - he said: Abu Mahdhurah informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Stand and call the adhan for prayer. So I stood before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he himself dictated the adhan to me, then he mentioned the like of the adhan in the first hadith. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this and said: Thus should the adhan be called. Others disagreed with them in two places: one of them is the beginning of the adhan, and they said: It should be said at the beginning of the adhan: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest."
"756","806 - And they argued for that with what Abu Bakrah and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us - and the wording is Abu Bakrah's - they said: Affan ibn Muslim al-Saffar narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Amir al-Ahwal narrated to us, he said: Makhul narrated to me that Abdillah ibn Muhayriz narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him the adhan as nineteen phrases: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, then he mentioned the rest of the adhan as in the first hadith."
"757","807 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us. (H). 808 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awaqi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us. (H). 809 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, they said: Hammam narrated to us, then they mentioned the like thereof with its chain. So in this hadith is that he says at the beginning of the adhan: Allah is Greatest, four times. So this view - in our opinion - is the sounder of the two views by reasoning; for we observe that part of the adhan is repeated in two places, and part of it is not repeated but mentioned in one place only. As for what is mentioned in one place and not repeated - the call to prayer and to success - each of them is proclaimed twice. And the testimony is mentioned in two places, at the beginning of the adhan and at its end; it is doubled at the beginning, so one says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, twice, then it is said singly at the end: There is no god but Allah, without doubling that. So whatever was doubled of the adhan was doubled upon half of what it is at the beginning. And the takbir is mentioned in two places, at the beginning of the adhan and after success. And they agreed that after success he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. So the reasoning, upon what we described, is that what is disputed, of the takbir with which the adhan is begun, should be like what is doubled, by analogy and reasoning upon what we explained of the testimony that there is no god but Allah. So what the adhan is begun with of takbir is double what is doubled thereof. So if what is doubled is: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, then what is begun with is its double: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. So this is the sound reasoning. And it is the view of Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him) and Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him) and Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on him). Except that there has also been narrated from Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him) on that the like of the first view. And the other place in which they differed thereof is tarji (repeating the testimony aloud after saying it softly); a group went to tarji and others left it."
"758","And they argued for that with what: 810 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umar ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that Abdillah ibn Zayd saw a man descend from the sky wearing two green garments - or two green cloaks - who stood on the stump of a wall and called: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. Then he mentioned the adhan as in the hadith of Abu Mahdhurah, except that he did not mention tarji. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: What you saw is good; teach it to Bilal."
"759","811 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: The companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me that Abdillah ibn Zayd al-Ansari saw the adhan in a dream, so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: Teach it to Bilal. So Bilal stood and called the adhan two by two. So this Abdillah ibn Zayd did not mention tarji in his hadith, so he disagreed with Abu Mahdhurah on tarji in the adhan. So it is possible that the tarji which Abu Mahdhurah reported was only because Abu Mahdhurah did not raise his voice with that, upon what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted from him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Go back and raise your voice. Such is the wording in the hadith. Since that is possible, reasoning became necessary, to extract thereby from the two views a sound view. So we observed that apart from what is disputed of the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, there is no tarji in it. So the reasoning upon that is that what they differed on thereof be referred to what they agreed upon, and their agreement that there is no tarji in the rest of the adhan besides the testimony rules over their disagreement on tarji in the testimony. And this which we described, and what we explained of negating tarji, is the view of Abu Hanifah (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on them both)."
"760","2 - Chapter of the iqamah, how is it? 812 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan ibn Mubashshir ibn Mukassir narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Bilal was commanded to double the adhan and to say the iqamah singly."
"761","813 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shubah and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"762","814 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"763","815 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Khalid, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"764","816 - Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Khalid, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"765","817 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abdillah al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Dinar al-Tahi narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: They had wanted to strike the bell and to raise a fire to announce prayer, until that man saw that dream, so Bilal was commanded to double the adhan and to say the iqamah singly."
"766","818 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr al-Jazari narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Bilal was commanded to double the adhan and to say the iqamah singly. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this and said: Thus the iqamah is said singly, once once. Others disagreed with them on one phrase thereof, and said: Except his saying: The prayer has begun, for he should double that twice. And they argued for that with what..."
"767","819 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Simak ibn Atiyyah, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Bilal was commanded to double the adhan and to say the iqamah singly, except the iqamah."
"768","820 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awaqi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"769","821 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ismail narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Bilal was commanded to double the adhan and to say the iqamah singly. Ismail said: So I narrated it to Ayyub and said to him: And to say the iqamah singly? He said: Except the iqamah."
"770","822 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Jafar al-Farra, from Muslim, a caller to prayer for the people of Kufa, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The adhan in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was twice twice, and the iqamah once once, except that when he said: The prayer has begun, he said it twice. So we knew it was the iqamah, then one of us would perform ablution and go out. And they also argued for that by reasoning, and said: We observe that of the adhan, what was repeated was not doubled the second time except that it was made half of what it is at the beginning. And the iqamah is not begun with; it only comes after the adhan. So the reasoning upon that is that what in it is also in the adhan be said singly, and what in it is not in the adhan be doubled. So all of the iqamah is in the adhan except: The prayer has begun. So he says all the iqamah singly, and doubles nothing but: The prayer has begun, for it is repeated because it is not in the adhan. Others disagreed with them on that and said: The whole iqamah is doubled, like the adhan exactly, except that at its end one says: The prayer has begun. And they said: What you mentioned from Bilal has been narrated from him contrary to that, of what we shall mention, Allah willing."
"771","823 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that Abdillah ibn Zayd saw a man descend from the sky wearing two green garments or two green cloaks, who stood on the stump of a wall and called the adhan: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, upon what we mentioned in the first chapter, then he sat, then he stood and called the iqamah the like of that. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: What you saw is good; teach it to Bilal."
"772","824 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: The companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that Abdillah ibn Zayd al-Ansari saw the adhan in a dream, so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: Teach it to Bilal. So he called the adhan two by two, and called the iqamah two by two, and sat for a sitting."
"773","825 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Our companions narrated to us, then he mentioned the like thereof. Abdillah said: Were it not that I suspect myself, I would have thought I saw that while awake, not asleep. Then he said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab said: By Allah, I myself was visited by the one who visited Abdillah, but when I saw that he had preceded me, I kept silent. So in this report is that Bilal called the adhan by the teaching of Abdillah ibn Zayd, by the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him with that, and he called the iqamah two by two. So this contradicts the first hadith. Then it has been narrated from Bilal that after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he would call the adhan two by two and call the iqamah two by two. So that also indicates the absence of what Anas narrated."
"774","826 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Bilal, that he would double the adhan and double the iqamah."
"775","827 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us. (H). 828 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman Luwayn narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: I heard Bilal call the adhan two by two and call the iqamah two by two. So this Bilal has had narrated from him on the iqamah what contradicts what Anas mentioned. And in the hadith of Abu Mahdhurah is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him the iqamah two by two."
"776","829 - Ali ibn Mabad and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Saib informed me, from his father and Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, she said: I heard Abu Mahdhurah. (H). 830 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Saib informed me, from his father and Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, that they heard Abu Mahdhurah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me the iqamah two by two: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah; I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer, come to prayer; come to success, come to success; the prayer has begun, the prayer has begun; Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; there is no god but Allah. Except that Abu Bakrah did not mention in his hadith: The prayer has begun."
"777","831 - Abu Bakrah and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Amir al-Ahwal narrated to me, he said: Makhul narrated to me that Abdillah ibn Muhayriz narrated to him that Abu Mahdhurah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him the iqamah as seventeen phrases: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Rawh exactly."
"778","832 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us. (H). 833 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Makhul, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Abu Mahdhurah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"779","834 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, they said: Hammam narrated to us. (H). 835 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Amir al-Ahwal narrated to us, he said: Makhul narrated to us that Ibn Muhayriz narrated to him that he heard Abu Mahdhurah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me the iqamah as seventeen phrases. So the sound understanding of the meanings of these reports necessitates that the iqamah be like the adhan exactly, upon what we mentioned; for there was disagreement about what Bilal was commanded thereof, then he himself afterwards remained upon doubling in the iqamah by succession of reports on that, so it was known that that is what he was commanded. And in the hadith of Abu Mahdhurah is doubling as well, so doubling in the iqamah is established. As for the aspect thereof by reasoning: a group of those who say the iqamah is said singly, once once, argued for that with the proof we mentioned for them in this chapter, of what is repeated in the adhan and what is not repeated; so the proof on that was that the adhan is as they mentioned. As for what thereof is mentioned in two places, it is doubled in the first place and said singly in the other place, and what thereof was not doubled is said singly. As for the iqamah, it is only done after the adhan has ended, so it has an independent ruling. And we observe that what the iqamah is concluded with, of the saying: There is no god but Allah, is what the adhan is also concluded with. So the reasoning upon that is that the rest of the iqamah be like the rest of the adhan as well. And among what enters upon this proof is that we observe what the iqamah is concluded with has no half, so it is possible that what is intended thereof is its half. Except that since it has no half, its ruling is the ruling of the rest of indivisible things, of which when part of them becomes obligatory, all of them become obligatory by its obligation. So for this reason, what the adhan and the iqamah are concluded with, of the saying: There is no god but Allah, is the same; so there was in that no proof for either of the two meanings over the other. Then we considered that, and we observed they did not disagree that in the iqamah, after prayer and success, he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, so he brings it here like what he brings in the adhan in this place as well, and does not bring it upon half of what it is in the adhan. So since this of the iqamah, which has a half, is like what it is in the adhan exactly, then what remains of the iqamah is also upon like what it is in the adhan exactly as well; nothing thereof is omitted. So it is established thereby that the iqamah is doubled, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on them). And that has also been narrated from a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"780","836 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi ibn Jariyah, from Ubayd, the freed slave of Salamah ibn al-Akwa, that Salamah ibn al-Akwa would double the iqamah."
"781","837 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Thawban would call the adhan two by two and call the iqamah two by two."
"782","838 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, he said: I heard Abu Mahdhurah call the adhan two by two and call the iqamah two by two. And there has been narrated from Mujahid on that what..."
"783","839 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Mujahid, on the iqamah once once: It is only something the rulers took lightly. So Mujahid informed that that is an innovation and that the origin is doubling."
"784","3 - Chapter of the caller's saying in the dawn adhan: Prayer is better than sleep. Abu Jafar said: A group disliked that there be said in the dawn adhan: Prayer is better than sleep. And they argued for that with the hadith of Abdillah ibn Zayd on the adhan which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to teach Bilal, so he commanded Bilal to call. Others disagreed with them on that, and held it good that that be said in the call for dawn after success. And among the proof for them on that was that even if that is not in the hadith of Abdillah ibn Zayd, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught it to Abu Mahdhurah after that, and commanded him to place it in the adhan for dawn. 840 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Saib informed me, from Umm Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, from Abu Mahdhurah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him in the first adhan of dawn: Prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep."
"785","841 - Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, he said: I heard Abu Mahdhurah say: I was a young boy, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Say: Prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep. Abu Jafar said: So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught that to Abu Mahdhurah, that was an addition to what is in the hadith of Abdillah ibn Zayd, and its use became obligatory. And the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him used that."
"786","842 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: In the first adhan, after success, was: Prayer is better than sleep, prayer is better than sleep."
"787","843 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us: Hushaym informed us. (H). 844 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Abu Awn, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Tathwib in the morning prayer was - when the caller said: Come to success, he said: Prayer is better than sleep, twice. So this Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) inform that that was of what the caller would call with in the dawn adhan. So what we mentioned is established thereby, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on them)."
"788","4 - Chapter of calling the adhan for dawn, what time is it? After dawn rises, or before that? 845 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Maslamah al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls. Ibn Shihab said: And he was a blind man who would not call until it was said to him: Morning has come, morning has come."
"789","846 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof, and he did not mention Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"790","847 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Salim, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"791","848 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain."
"792","849 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim ibn Abdillah said: I heard Abdillah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls."
"793","850 - al-Hasan ibn Abdillah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"794","851 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"795","852 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"796","853 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik and Shubah narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof. Except that he said: Until Bilal or Ibn Umm Maktum calls - Shubah doubted."
"797","854 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from al-Qasim, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof, without doubt. She said: And there was between the two only the span of this one descending and this one ascending."
"798","855 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman narrate from his aunt Unaysah that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal or Ibn Umm Maktum calls at night, so eat and drink until Bilal or Ibn Umm Maktum calls. So when this one would descend and this one wanted to ascend, they would cling to him and say: Stay as you are until we take sahur."
"799","856 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain, with the addition: And she had performed Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there was between the two only the span of this one ascending and this one descending."
"800","857 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his aunt Unaysah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibn Umm Maktum calls the adhan at night, so eat and drink until you hear the call of Bilal."
"801","858 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Sawadah al-Qushayri - and he was their imam - he said: I heard Samurah ibn Jundub say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let neither the call of Bilal nor this whiteness deceive you, until dawn appears, or dawn breaks."
"802","859 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sawadah al-Qushayri, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to the view that the adhan for dawn is called before its time enters, and they argued for that with these reports. Among those who went to that is Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him). Others disagreed with them on that and said: The adhan for dawn too should not be called except after its time enters, just as the adhan for the rest of the prayers is not called except after their times enter. And they argued for that and said: The adhan of Bilal which he would call at night was for other than prayer. So they mentioned what..."
"803","860 - Ali ibn Mabad and Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, they said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us - and the wording is Ibn Mabad's. (H). 861 - And Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us. (H). 862 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us. (H). 863 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abdillah ibn Masud, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let not the adhan of Bilal prevent any of you from his sahur, for he calls - or calls the adhan - so that your absent one may return and your standing one may wake. And he said: Dawn, or morning, is not like this and like this. And he joined his fingers and separated them. And in the hadith of Zuhayr specifically: And Zuhayr raised his hand and lowered it until he said like this, and Zuhayr stretched his hands out wide. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that that call of Bilal was so that the sleeper would wake and the absent one return, not for prayer."
"804","And there has been narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) what: Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (H). 864 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that Bilal called the adhan before dawn rose, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to return and call: Verily the servant has slept! So he returned and called: Verily the servant has slept! So this Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned, and he is of those who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls. So it is established thereby that what of his call before dawn rose, of what was permitted for him, was for other than prayer, and that what he disapproved of him when he did it before dawn was for prayer. And there has also been narrated from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her), what..."
"805","865 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her), daughter of Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the caller called the adhan for dawn, would stand and pray the two rakahs of dawn, then go out to the mosque, and food became forbidden. And they would not call the adhan until morning came. So this Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) informs from Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her) that they would not call the adhan for prayer except after dawn rose. And the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Bilal to return and call: Verily the servant has slept! - indicates that their custom was that they knew no adhan before dawn. Had they known that as adhan, they would not have needed this call. And he intended by it, in our view - and Allah knows best - with that call only to inform them that they are still in night; so that whoever of them preferred to pray would pray, and would not refrain from what the fasting one refrains from. And it is possible that Bilal would call the adhan at a time in which he thought dawn had risen, without verifying that, due to weak eyesight."
"806","And the proof of that is what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishkab narrated to us. (H). 866 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Shihab ibn Abbad al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let not the adhan of Bilal deceive you, for there is something in his eyesight. So that indicated that Bilal intended dawn but missed it due to weak eyesight. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them not to act upon his adhan, since error was of his habits due to weak eyesight. And there has..."
"807","867 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah narrated to us, from Salim, from Sulayman, from Ibn Uthman, that he narrated to him, from Adi ibn Hatim, from Abu Dharr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Bilal: You call the adhan when dawn is shining bright, but that is not morning; morning is only like this, spread wide. So he informed him in this report that he would call the adhan at the rising of what he saw as dawn, though it was not - in reality - dawn. And we have narrated from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bilal calls at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls. She said: And there was between the two only the span of this one ascending and this one descending. So since there was between their two adhans the closeness we mentioned, it is established that both intended one time, which is the rising of dawn; so Bilal missed it due to what he saw, and Ibn Umm Maktum hit it because he would not do it until the congregation said to him: Morning has come, morning has come. Then there has been narrated from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what..."
"808","869 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, he said: I said: O Mother of the Believers, when do you pray witr? She said: When the caller calls. al-Aswad said: And they would only call after morning. And this was their call in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for al-Aswad's hearing from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) was in Madinah. And she had heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we narrated from her thereof, yet she did not object to their leaving the call before dawn, nor did anyone else of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) object to that. So that indicated that Bilal's intent with that adhan of his was dawn, and that the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): So eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls, is only for hitting the rising of dawn. So when these reports were narrated upon what we mentioned, and it was in the hadith of Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her) that they would not call until dawn rose - if that is so, then the meaning to which Abu Yusuf went is nullified. And if the meaning is otherwise, and they would call before dawn intending that, then the hadith of Ibn Masud from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has explained that that call was for other than prayer. And in the call of Ibn Umm Maktum after dawn rose is proof that that is the place of adhan for that prayer. Had that not been the place of adhan for it, the adhan would not have been permitted therein. So when that was permitted, it is established that that time is a time for the adhan, and their advancing Bilal's adhan before that bears what we mentioned. Then we considered that also by reasoning, to extract from the two views a sound view. So we observed that the rest of the prayers, besides dawn, are not called for except after their times enter. And they differed on dawn: a group said the call for it is before its time enters, and others said rather it is after its time enters. So the reasoning, upon what we described, is that the adhan for it be like the adhan for the rest of the prayers. So since that is after their times enter, it is likewise so for dawn. So this is the reasoning, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah (may Allah be pleased with him), and Muhammad, and Sufyan al-Thawri."
"809","870 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan ibn Said, and a man said to him: I call the adhan before dawn rises, to be the first to knock on the door of heaven with the call. So Sufyan said: No, until dawn breaks. And there has been narrated from Alqamah something of this."
"810","871 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Ali ibn Ali, from Ibrahim, he said: We accompanied Alqamah to Makkah, then he went out at night and heard a caller calling at night, so he said: As for this one, he has opposed the practice of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); had he been asleep it would have been better for him. Then when dawn rose, he called. So Alqamah informed that calling before dawn rises is contrary to the practice of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"811","5 - Chapter: Two men, one of them calls the adhan and the other calls the iqamah. 872 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum informed me, from Ziyad ibn Nuaym, that he heard Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Sudai say: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when it was the beginning of morning he commanded me and I called the adhan. Then he stood for prayer and Bilal came to call the iqamah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The brother of Suda called the adhan, and whoever calls the adhan, he calls the iqamah."
"812","873 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad informed me, from Ziyad ibn Nuaym, from Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Sudai, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this hadith and said: One other than the one who called the adhan for prayer should not call the iqamah for it. Others disagreed with them on that and said: There is no harm in one other than the one who called the adhan for it calling the iqamah for prayer."
"813","And they argued for that with what: 874 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb informed me, from Abu al-Umays, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Zayd, from his father, from his grandfather, that when he was shown the adhan, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Bilal and he called the adhan, then he commanded Abdillah and he called the iqamah."
"814","875 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Abu al-Umays, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Zayd, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him how I saw the adhan, and he said: Cast them upon Bilal, for he is more melodious in voice than you. So when Bilal called the adhan, Abdillah regretted, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to call the iqamah. So when these two hadiths conflicted, we wanted to seek the ruling of this chapter by reasoning, to extract thereby from the two views a sound view. So we considered that, and we found the agreed-upon principle that two men should not call one adhan, each of them calling part of it. So it was possible that the adhan and the iqamah be likewise, none but one man doing them. And it was possible that they be like two separate things, so there is no harm in each of them being undertaken by a man separately. So we considered that, and we observed that prayer has preceding causes, of calling to it with the adhan, and of the iqamah for it - this in the rest of the prayers. And we observed that Friday is preceded by a sermon which is indispensable; so prayer was bound with the sermon, and whoever prayed Friday without a sermon, his prayer is invalid, until the sermon has preceded prayer. And we observed that the imam need not be other than the preacher, since each of them is bound with its companion. So since both were indispensable, none but one man should undertake them. And we observed that the iqamah was also made of the causes of prayer, and they agreed that there is no harm in one other than the imam undertaking it. So just as one other than the imam would undertake it, though it is closer to prayer than to the adhan, there is no harm in one other than the one who undertakes the adhan undertaking it. So this is the reasoning, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah have mercy on them)."
"815","6 - Chapter of what is recommended for a man to say when he hears the adhan. 876 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear the caller - and in the hadith of Malik: the call - then say the like of what he says; and in the hadith of Malik: what the caller says."
"816","877 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, from Yunus, then he mentioned the like thereof."
"817","878 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zurah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: Kab ibn Alqamah informed us that he heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, the freed slave of Nafi ibn Abd Amr al-Qurashi, say: He heard Abdillah ibn Amr ibn al-As say: He heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear the caller, say the like of what he says, then send blessings upon me; for whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah sends ten upon him thereby. Then ask Allah for me al-Wasilah, for it is a station in Paradise befitting none but a servant of the servants of Allah, and I hope that I am he. So whoever asks Allah for me al-Wasilah, intercession becomes due for him."
"818","879 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. (H). 880 - And Ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Abu al-Malih, from Abdillah ibn Utbah, from Umm Habibah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he heard the caller, would say the like of what he says, until he fell silent."
"819","881 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr al-Laythi narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: We were with Muawiyah, and the caller called, so Muawiyah said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When you hear the caller calling, say the like of his saying, or as he said. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to these reports and said: Whoever hears the adhan should say as the caller says, until he finishes his adhan. Others disagreed with them on that and said: His saying: Come to prayer, come to success, has no meaning as repetition, for the caller only says that to call people thereby to prayer and to success. And the hearer does not say what he says thereof by way of calling people to that; he only says it by way of remembrance, and this is not of remembrance. So he should place in its stead what has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the other reports, which is: There is no power nor strength except with Allah. And among the proof for them on that was that it is possible that his saying: So say the like of what he says, until he falls silent, means: So say the like of what he began the adhan with, of takbir and the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, until he falls silent. So the takbir and the testimony are what is intended by his saying: The like of what he says. And he intended that in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah."
"820","882 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Ishaq, from Ibn Shihab. (H). 883 - And Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the caller testifies, say the like of what he says. As for what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying at that: There is no power nor strength except with Allah, and of urging upon that..."
"821","884 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hafs ibn Asim, from his father, from his grandfather Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the caller says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, and one of you says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; then he says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and he says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah; then he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and he says: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; then he says: Come to prayer, and he says: There is no power nor strength except with Allah; then he says: Come to success, and he says: There is no power nor strength except with Allah; then he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, and he says: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; then he says: There is no god but Allah, and he says: There is no god but Allah, from his heart, he enters Paradise."
"822","885 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Sharik, from Asim ibn Ubaydillah, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Abu Rafi, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the caller, he would say the like of what he said, and when he said: Come to prayer, come to success, he would say: There is no power nor strength except with Allah."
"823","886 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Qurashi, from Isa ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, he said: We were with Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, and the caller called, saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. So Muawiyah said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. Then he said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, so Muawiyah said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. Then he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, so Muawiyah said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, until he reached: Come to prayer, come to success. Then he said: There is no power nor strength except with Allah. 887 - Yahya said: And a man narrated to me that when Muawiyah said that, he said: Thus did we hear your Prophet say."
"824","888 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, that Muawiyah said the like of that, then said: Thus said the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"825","889 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Attar - meaning Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman - narrated to me, from Amr ibn Yahya, from Isa ibn Umar, from Abdillah ibn Alqamah, from his father, he said: I was sitting beside Muawiyah, then he mentioned the like thereof. Then Muawiyah said: Thus did I hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say."
"826","890 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Yahya al-Ansari informed me that Isa ibn Muhammad informed him, from Abdillah ibn Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from his father, then he mentioned the like thereof. And there has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would say at the adhan, and command with it, what..."
"827","891 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Hukaym ibn Abdillah ibn Qays, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, from Sad, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever says when he hears the caller: And I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, alone without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger; I am pleased with Allah as Lord and with Islam as religion, his sin is forgiven."
"828","892 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like thereof."
"829","893 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Mughirah, from al-Hukaym ibn Abdillah ibn Qays, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain, with the addition that he said: Whoever says when he hears the caller testifying..."
"830","894 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Bazzar narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abdillah ibn Masud, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim who says when he hears the call: so the caller declares greatness and he declares greatness, then he testifies that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and he testifies to that, then he says: O Allah, give Muhammad al-Wasilah, and place his rank among the highest, and his love among the chosen, and his abode among the near ones - but intercession of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Resurrection becomes due for him."
"831","895 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the caller, he would say: O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and the established prayer, give our master Muhammad al-Wasilah, and raise him to the praised station which You promised him."
"832","896 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym al-Tahhan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Hafsah bint Abi Kathir, from her father, she said: Umm Salamah taught me, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me, he said: O Umm Salamah, when it is at the Maghrib adhan, say: O Allah, this is at the coming of Your night and the departing of Your day, and the voices of Your callers, and the presence of Your prayer - forgive me. So these reports indicate that what is said at the adhan is intended as remembrance; so all of the adhan is remembrance except: Come to prayer, come to success, for they are a call. So whatever of the adhan is remembrance, the hearer should say it; and whatever thereof is a call to prayer, then the remembrance which is other than it is better than it and more deserving to be said. And a group said: The saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When you hear the caller, say the like of what he says, is upon obligation. Others disagreed with them on that and said: That is upon recommendation, not upon obligation. And among the proof for them on that was what..."
"833","897 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Alqamah, from Abdillah, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys, and he heard a caller saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon the fitrah. Then he said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He has come out of the Fire. He said: So we rushed to him, and behold he was a herdsman whom prayer had overtaken, so he called with it. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the caller calling and said other than what he said. So that indicated that his saying: When you hear the caller, say the like of what he says, is not upon obligation, and that it is upon recommendation and encouragement to good and attaining merit, as he taught people of the supplication which he commanded them to say after prayer and the like thereof."
"834","7 - Chapter of the times of prayer. 898 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Rabiah, from Hakim ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"835","899 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith al-Makhzumi, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"836","900 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Rabiah, from Hakim ibn Hakim, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) led me twice at the door of the House. He prayed with me Zuhr when the sun declined, and prayed with me Asr when the shadow of everything became like it, and prayed with me Maghrib when the fasting one breaks fast, and prayed with me Isha when the twilight disappeared, and prayed with me Fajr when food and drink became forbidden for the fasting one. And he prayed with me Zuhr the next day when the shadow of everything became like it, and prayed with me Asr when the shadow of everything became twice it, and prayed with me Maghrib when the fasting one breaks fast, and prayed with me Isha when a third of the night passed, and prayed with me the morning prayer when it was light. Then he turned to me and said: O Muhammad, the time is between these two times; this is the time of the prophets before you."
"837","901 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Luhayah narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said ibn Suwayd al-Saidi; he heard Abu Said al-Khudri say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) led me in prayer. He prayed Zuhr when the sun declined, and prayed Asr when a standing object stood, and prayed Maghrib when the sun set, and prayed Isha when the twilight disappeared, and prayed dawn when dawn rose. Then he led me the second day: he prayed Zuhr when the shade of everything was like it, and prayed Asr when the shade was two standings, and prayed Maghrib when the sun set, and prayed the last Isha up to the first third of the night, and prayed dawn when the sun was about to rise. Then he said: Prayer is between these two times."
"838","902 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is Jibril (peace be upon him) teaching you your religion. Then he mentioned the like thereof, except that he said of the last Isha: And he prayed it the second day when an hour of the night had passed."
"839","903 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: A man asked the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the time of prayer, and he said: Pray with me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed dawn when dawn rose, then prayed Zuhr when the sun declined, then prayed Asr when the shade of a man was like him, then prayed Maghrib when the sun set, then prayed Isha before the twilight disappeared, then prayed dawn and made it light, then prayed Zuhr when the shade of a man was like him, then prayed Asr when the shade of a man was twice him, then prayed Maghrib before the twilight disappeared, then prayed Isha. Some of them said: A third of the night; and some of them said: Half the night."
"840","904 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah say: A man of them narrated to me that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about the times of prayer, so he commanded him to witness prayer with him. He prayed dawn early, then prayed Zuhr early, then prayed Asr early, then prayed Maghrib early, then prayed Isha early, then prayed all the prayers the next day late. Then he said to the man: What is between my two prayers in these two times is all time."
"841","905 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Badr ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Musa narrated to me, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A questioner came to him and asked him about the times of prayer, but he did not answer him anything. He commanded Bilal and he established Fajr when dawn split and people could hardly recognize one another. Then he commanded him and he established Zuhr when the sun declined while one saying would say: Midday has come or not; and he was more knowledgeable than them. Then he commanded him and he established Asr while the sun was high, then he commanded him and he established Maghrib when the sun fell, then he commanded him and he established Isha when the twilight disappeared. Then he delayed Fajr the next day until he left it while one saying would say: The sun has risen or nearly. Then he delayed Zuhr until it was near Asr, then delayed Asr until he left it while one saying would say: The sun has reddened, then delayed Maghrib until it was at the falling of the twilight, then delayed Isha until it was two-thirds of the first night. Then in the morning he called the questioner and said: The time is between these two."
"842","906 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man asked him about the time of prayer, and he said: Pray with us. He said: When the sun declined he commanded Bilal and he called the adhan, then he commanded him and he established Asr while the sun was white, high and clear, then he commanded him and he established Maghrib when the sun set, then he commanded him and he established Isha when the twilight disappeared, then he commanded him and he established Fajr when dawn rose. The next day he commanded him and he called the adhan for Zuhr, cooling it, doing well to cool it; and he prayed Asr while the sun was high, delaying it beyond what it was; and he prayed Maghrib before the twilight disappeared; and he prayed Isha after a third of the night had gone; and he prayed Fajr making it light. Then he said: Where is the questioner about the time of prayer? The man said: I am, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Your time of prayer is between what you saw. As for what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports on the Fajr prayer: they did not disagree from him therein that he prayed it the first day when dawn rose, which is its first time, and prayed it the next day when the sun was about to rise. And this is the agreement of the Muslims that the first time of Fajr is when dawn rises and its last time is when the sun rises. As for what was mentioned from him on the Zuhr prayer: it was mentioned from him that he prayed it when the sun declined; upon that is the agreement of the Muslims that that is its first time. As for its last time: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him), and Jabir, and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from him that he prayed it the next day when the shadow of everything was like it. So it is possible that that was after the shadow of everything became like it, so that would still be Zuhr time. And it is possible that that was close to the shadow of everything becoming like it, and this is permissible in language. Allah Almighty said: When you divorce women and they reach their term, retain them in kindness or release them in kindness. That retaining and releasing was not intended to be done after the term is reached, for after the term is reached she has separated and it is forbidden for him to retain her. And Allah Almighty explained that in another place, saying: When you divorce women and they reach their term, do not prevent them from marrying their husbands. So Allah Almighty informed that it is lawful for them after their term is reached to marry. So it is established thereby that what was given to husbands over them in the other verse is only close to reaching the term, not after reaching the term. Likewise what was narrated from those we mentioned, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he prayed Zuhr the second day when the shadow of everything became like it, is possible to be close to the shadow of everything becoming like it. So when the shadow becomes like it, Zuhr time has exited. And the proof of what we mentioned thereof is that those who mentioned this from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also mentioned from him in these reports that he prayed Asr the first day when the shadow of everything became like it, then said: What is between these two is time. So it is impossible that what is between them be time while he combined them in one time; but its meaning, in our view - and Allah knows best - is what we mentioned. And what is in the hadith of Abu Musa also indicated that: he said of what he reported of his prayer the second day: Then he delayed Zuhr until it was near Asr. So he informed that he only prayed it that day close to the entering of Asr time, not in Asr time. So it is established thereby, when they agreed in these narrations that after the shadow of everything becomes like it is a time for Asr, that it is impossible for it to be a time for Zuhr, due to his informing that the time of each prayer is between his two prayers in the two days. And what also indicated that is what..."
"843","907 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Prayer has a first and a last; the first time of Zuhr is when the sun declines, and its last time is when Asr time enters. So it is established thereby that the entering of Asr time is after the exiting of Zuhr time. As for what was mentioned from him on the Asr prayer: it was not disagreed from him that he prayed it the first day in the time we mentioned from him, so it is established that that is its first time. And it was mentioned from him that he prayed it the second day when the shadow of everything became twice it, then said: The time is between these two. So it is possible that that is its last time which, when it exits, it is missed. And it is possible that it is the time beyond which prayer should not be delayed, and that whoever prays it after it, though he prayed it in its time, is negligent, for what of its time wherein is merit has passed him, though it has not yet been missed. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: A man prays prayer without missing it, yet what passed him of its time is better for him than his family and wealth. So it is established thereby that prayer in a particular part of the time is better than prayer in the rest of that time. And it is possible that the time beyond which Asr should not be delayed until it exits is this time in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it the second day. And what indicated what we mentioned is what..."
"844","908 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Prayer has a first and a last; the first time of Asr is when its time enters, and its last time is when the sun yellows."
"845","909 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Ayyub, from Abdillah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The time of Asr is so long as the sun has not yellowed."
"846","910 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Ayyub, from Abdillah ibn Amr. Shubah said: He narrated it to me three times; once he raised it and twice he did not raise it, then he mentioned the like thereof. So in this report is that its last time is when the sun yellows, and that is after the shadow becomes two standings. So that indicated that the time which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended in the first reports of its time is the time of merit, not the time which, when it exits, prayer is missed by its exiting, so that these reports be sound and not conflict. Except that a group went to the view that its last time is until sunset. And they argued for that with what..."
"847","911 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever catches a rakah of the dawn prayer before the sun rises has caught the prayer, and whoever catches two rakahs of the Asr prayer before the sun sets has caught it."
"848","912 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us; Muhammad informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"849","913 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, and Busr ibn Said and Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever catches a rakah of dawn before the sun rises has caught dawn, and whoever catches a rakah of Asr before the sun sets has caught Asr."
"850","914 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof. They said: Since whoever caught of Asr what we mentioned in these reports is one who caught it, it is established that its last time is sunset. And among those who held that are Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan (may Allah have mercy on them). And among the proof of those who went to the view that its last time is until the sun changes was what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding prayer at sunset. Of that is what..."
"851","915 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Dharr, he said: Abdillah said to me: We used to be forbidden from prayer at sunrise, and at its setting, and at midday."
"852","916 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer when the horn of the sun rises or the horn of the sun sets."
"853","917 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi narrated to us, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, he said: Three hours in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid us to pray and to bury our dead: when the sun rises shining until it ascends, and when the midday stander stands until it declines, and when the sun inclines to setting until it sets."
"854","918 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Musab narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Salim ibn Abdillah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not intend with your prayer sunrise nor sunset. When the edge of the sun appears, delay prayer until it shines forth; and when the edge of the sun disappears, delay prayer until it disappears."
"855","919 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like thereof."
"856","920 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Let none of you intend and pray at sunrise, nor at its setting."
"857","921 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) erred; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade intending sunrise or sunset."
"858","922 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih informed me, he said: Abu Yahya, and Damrah ibn Habib, and Abu Talhah narrated to me, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, he said: Amr ibn Abasah narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the sun rises, it rises between the two horns of Satan, and it is the hour of the prayer of the disbelievers; so leave prayer until it ascends and its rays depart. Then prayer is attended and witnessed until midday; for it is an hour in which the doors of Hell are opened and it is stoked. So leave prayer until the shade turns. Then prayer is attended and witnessed until sunset; for it sets between the two horns of Satan, and it is the hour of the prayer of the disbelievers."
"859","923 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, he said: I heard al-Muhallab ibn Abi Sufrah narrate from Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not pray at sunrise nor at its setting, for it rises between the two horns of Satan - or upon the two horns of Satan - and sets between the two horns of Satan - or upon the two horns of Satan. They said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer at sunset, it is established that it is not a time of prayer and that Asr time exits with its entering. And among the proof of the others against him was that in this hadith is the prohibition of prayer at sunset, while in another is narrated: Whoever catches a rakah of Asr before the sun sets has caught Asr. So in that is permission to enter Asr in that time. So he placed the prohibition in the first hadith upon other than what was permitted in the other hadith, so that the two hadiths not conflict. So this is the best construction of the reports, so that they not conflict. As for the aspect of reasoning in our view thereof: we observe that Zuhr time - and all prayers therein are permitted: all voluntary, and making up every missed prayer. Likewise what is agreed upon that it is Asr time, and dawn time - making up missed prayers therein is permitted; so he only forbade voluntary prayer specifically therein. So every time agreed upon that it is a time of prayer of these prayers, all have agreed that a missed prayer is made up therein. So when it is established that this is the description of the agreed-upon prayer times, and it is established that at sunset no missed prayer is made up, by their agreement, its description thereby exited the description of the prescribed prayer times. And it is established that no prayer at all is prayed therein, like midday and sunrise, and that the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prayer at sunset abrogates his saying: Whoever catches of Asr a rakah before the sun sets has caught Asr, due to the proofs we explained and clarified. So this is the reasoning in our view, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him), and Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him) and Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on him). As for Maghrib time: in all the first reports is that he prayed it at sunset. And a group went contrary to that and said: The first time of Maghrib is when the star rises. And they argued for that with what..."
"860","924 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Khayr ibn Nuaym, from Abu Hubayrah al-Sabai, from Abu Tamim al-Jayshani, from Abu Basrah al-Ghifari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us Asr at al-Mukhammas, and said: This prayer was presented to those before you and they neglected it. Whoever of you guards it is given his reward twice. And there is no prayer after it until the witness rises."
"861","925 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Khayr ibn Nuaym al-Hadrami, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain, except that he did not mention: at al-Mukhammas. And he said: There is no prayer after it until the witness is seen; and the witness is the star. So they said: The rising of the star is its first time. And his saying, in our view: And there is no prayer after it until the witness is seen, is possible to be the last saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as al-Layth mentioned it, and the witness be the night. But the one whom other than al-Layth narrated interpreted that the witness is the star, so he said that by his opinion, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have reached succession that he would pray Maghrib when the sun was veiled by the screen."
"862","926 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Umarah, from Abu Atiyyah, he said: Masruq and I entered upon Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and Masruq said: O Mother of the Believers, two men of the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), both not falling short in good. As for one of them, he hastens Maghrib and hastens breaking fast, and the other delays Maghrib until the stars appear, and delays breaking fast - meaning Abu Musa. She said: Which of them hastens prayer and breaking fast? He said: Abdillah. Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: Thus would the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"863","927 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, he said: Bashir ibn Abi Masud informed me, from Abu Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Maghrib when the sun set."
"864","928 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Hasan, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Maghrib when the sun set."
"865","929 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa, he said: We would pray Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when it was veiled by the screen. And there has also been narrated on that from those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"866","930 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: Umar said: Pray this prayer - meaning Maghrib - while the mountain passes are bright."
"867","931 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Imran, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain."
"868","932 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Imran, then he mentioned the like thereof with its chain."
"869","933 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from al-Muhajir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Abu Musa: Pray Maghrib when the sun sets."
"870","934 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to the people of al-Jabiyah: Pray Maghrib before the stars appear."
"871","935 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, he said: Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah led his companions in the Maghrib prayer, then his companions stood watching the sun, so he said: What are you looking at? They said: We are watching whether the sun has set. So Abdullah said: This, by Allah besides whom there is no god, is the time of this prayer. Then Abdullah recited: Establish prayer from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night, and he pointed with his hand toward the west and said: This is the darkness of the night, and he pointed with his hand toward the east and said: This is the decline of the sun. It was said: Did Umarah also narrate to you? He said: Yes."
"872","936 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid said: Ibn Masud led his companions in Maghrib when the sun set, then he said: This - by the One besides whom there is no god - is the time of this prayer."
"873","937 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, he said: Abdullah ibn Murrah narrated to me, from Masruq, from Abdullah, the same."
"874","938 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, that he said when the sun set: By the One besides whom there is no god, this hour is indeed the appointed time of this prayer. Then Abdullah recited as confirmation of that from the Book of Allah: Establish prayer from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night. He said: Its decline is when it sets, and the darkness of the night is when it becomes dark; so the prayer is between them."
"875","939 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Abdur Rahman ibn Nafi ibn Labiba, he said: Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: When is the darkness of the night? He said: When the sun sets. He said: Then hasten Maghrib right after it, then hasten it right after it."
"876","940 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abdur Rahman, he said: I saw Umar and Uthman praying Maghrib in Ramadan when he looked toward the black night, then they would break fast afterwards. These are the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who did not differ that the beginning of the time of Maghrib is when the sun sets. And this is also the reasoning; for we have seen the entering of the day as a time for the Fajr prayer, so likewise the entering of the night is a time for the Maghrib prayer. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, and the generality of the jurists. And the people differed about the end of the time of Maghrib. A group said: When the twilight - which is the redness - disappears, its time ends, and among those who said that are Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And others said: When the twilight - which is the whiteness after the redness - disappears, its time ends, and among those who said that is Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him. And the reasoning in that, in our view, is that they agreed that the redness before the whiteness is part of its time, and their disagreement is only about the whiteness after it. Some said: Its ruling is the ruling of the redness, and some said: Its ruling is different from the ruling of the redness. So we examined that and saw that before Fajr there is redness followed by the whiteness of dawn, and the redness and whiteness in that case were a time for one prayer, which is Fajr, and when both end its time ends. So by analogy, the whiteness and redness in Maghrib are also a time for one prayer and their ruling is one ruling; when both end, the prayer times which are its time end. As for the last Isha, all those reports state that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it on the first day after the twilight disappeared, except Jabir ibn Abdullah, for he mentioned that he prayed it before the twilight disappeared. That, in our view - and Allah knows best - can mean that Jabir meant by twilight the whiteness, while the others meant by twilight the redness, so he prayed it after the disappearance of the redness and before the disappearance of the whiteness, so that these reports are all sound and do not contradict. And the establishment of what we mentioned indicates what some of them said: that after the disappearance of the redness is still Maghrib time until the whiteness disappears. As for the end of the time of the last Isha, Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Abu Said al-Khudri and Abu Musa mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed it until a third of the night, then prayed it. And Jabir ibn Abdullah said he prayed it at a time - some said it is a third of the night, and some said it is half the night. It is possible he prayed it before a third had passed, so the passing of a third would be the end of its time. And it is possible he prayed it after a third, so some of its time remained after the passing of a third. Since that was possible, we looked into what was narrated about that."
"877","941 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Prayer has a beginning and an end. The beginning of the time of Isha is when the horizon disappears, and the end of its time is when the night reaches its middle. The beginning of the time of Fajr is when dawn rises, and the end of its time is when the sun rises."
"878","942 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The time of Isha is until half the night."
"879","943 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Amr. Shuba said: He narrated it to me three times, once raised to the Prophet and twice not raised. He mentioned the same. So it is established by these reports that what is after a third of the night is also part of the time of the last Isha. And there has also been narrated about that what indicates that."
"880","944 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: One night we waited for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the last Isha, then he came out to us when a third of the night had gone, or after it, and we do not know whether something occupied him with his family or otherwise. When he came out he said: You are waiting for a prayer which no people of any religion besides you are waiting for. Had it not been hardship upon my nation, I would have prayed with them at this hour. Then he ordered the caller to prayer, who gave the iqama, and he prayed."
"881","945 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Zaida, from Sulayman, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prepared an army, until when the night reached its middle, or reached that, he came out to us and said: The people have prayed and slept, while you are waiting for this prayer. You will remain in prayer as long as you wait for it."
"882","946 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamza informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, that Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the night prayer one night until Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, called him and said: The people and the children have slept. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and said: No one among the people of the earth waits for it besides you, and it was not prayed on that day except in Medina. She said: And they used to pray the night prayer between the disappearance of the darkness of the night and a third of the night."
"883","947 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the night prayer until near half the night. When he prayed, he turned to us with his face and said: The people have prayed and slept, and you have remained in prayer as long as you waited for it."
"884","948 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, he said: Thabit informed us that they asked Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have a ring? He said: Yes. Then he said: He delayed Isha one night until nearly half the night had gone, or until half the night, then he mentioned the same. These reports show that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Isha after a third of the night had passed, so it is established that the passing of a third of the night does not end its time. But the meaning of that - in our view, and Allah knows best - is that the most virtuous time of the last Isha in which it is prayed is from when the twilight disappears until a third of the night, which is the time in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray it, as we mentioned in the hadith of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her. Then what is after that until half the night passes is lesser in virtue, so that these reports do not contradict. Then we wanted to see whether anything of its time remains after half the night passes."
"885","949 - So we examined that, and Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub, Abdullah ibn Umar, and Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from Humayd al-Tawil, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the prayer one night until half the night, then he finished and turned to us with his face after he had prayed with us. He said: The people have prayed and slept, and you have remained in prayer as long as you waited for it."
"886","950 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, the same."
"887","951 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. These reports show that he prayed it after half the night had passed, which is proof that some of its time remained after half the night. And there has also been narrated from him on that what is even clearer than this."
"888","952 - Ali ibn Mabad and Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, they said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Mughira ibn Hakim informed me, from Umm Kulthum bint Abi Bakr, that she informed him from Aisha, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed one night until most of the night had gone, and until the people of the mosque had slept, then he came out and prayed, and said: It is indeed its time, were it not that I would burden my nation. This shows he prayed it after most of the night had passed, and he informed that that is a time for it. So it is established by verifying these reports that the beginning of the time of the last Isha is from when the twilight disappears until the whole night passes, but it is in three periods: from when its time enters until a third of the night passes is the most virtuous time in which it is prayed. From after that until half the night is complete is lesser in virtue. And after half the night is lesser in virtue than all before it. And there has also been narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about its time what indicates what we mentioned."
"889","953 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Aslam, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote: The time of the last Isha is when the twilight disappears until a third of the night, and do not delay it until then except one who is occupied, and do not sleep before it; whoever sleeps before it, may his eyes not sleep. He said it three times."
"890","This is Umar, and there has also been narrated from him what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from al-Muhajir, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abu Musa: Pray the Isha prayer from the evening until half the night, whenever you wish."
"891","954 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from al-Muhajir, the same."
"892","955 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad, from al-Muhajir, the same, and he added: I do not know that except as half for you. This shows he allowed him to pray it until half the night, and he counted that as half. And there has also been narrated from him about that what:"
"893","956 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit. 957 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Nafi ibn Jubayr, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abu Musa: Pray Isha whenever of the night you wish, but do not neglect it. This shows he made the whole night a time for it, provided he does not neglect it. Its explanation - in our view - is that leaving it until half the night is neglecting it, while leaving it until a third of the night passes is not neglecting it; rather he is held to the virtue sought in praying it early in its time, and what is between these two times is midway between the two matters, meaning it is below the first time and above the second. This also agrees with the meaning we gave to what we mentioned before of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"894","958 - And there has been narrated from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, about that from his saying what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us. 959 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ubayd ibn Jurayj, that he said to Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him: What is exceeding the Isha prayer? He said: The rising of dawn. So Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, made its limit by which it is missed the rising of dawn. And we narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed Isha on the second day - when asked about the prayer times - after an hour of the night had passed. And in his hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: The time of Isha is until half the night. So it is established that its time is until the rising of dawn, but parts of it are more virtuous than others. And all the sayings we explained in this chapter are the saying of Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on him, except what we explained of their disagreement about the time of Zuhr. 960 - For Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, said: It is until the shadow becomes twice, thus Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated from him, in what:"
"895","961 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me, from Ibn al-Thalji, from al-Hasan ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hanifa, may Allah have mercy on him, that he said about that: The end of its time is when the shadow becomes equal to the object, and this is the saying of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and Muhammad, and we take this view."
"896","8 - Chapter on combining two prayers, how is it? 962 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Hudhayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to combine two prayers while traveling."
"897","963 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Abu al-Tufayl, that Muadh ibn Jabal informed him that they went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Tabuk, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha."
"898","964 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to me, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Qurra ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, he said: Abu al-Tufayl narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, and he mentioned the same. He said: I said: What moved him to do that? He said: He wanted not to burden his nation."
"899","965 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Zayd narrating from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed eight together and seven together."
"900","966 - Ismail ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Zayd informed us that he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: I prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Medina eight together and seven together. I said to Abu al-Shatha: I think he delayed Zuhr and hastened Asr, and delayed Maghrib and hastened Isha. He said: And I think that too."
"901","967 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us Zuhr and Asr together, and Maghrib and Isha together, without fear or travel."
"902","968 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Qurra narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, and he mentioned the same with its chain. I said: What moved him to do that? He said: He wanted not to burden his nation."
"903","969 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"904","970 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslama al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays al-Farra narrated to us, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawama, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the same, except he said: Without travel or rain."
"905","971 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, delayed the Maghrib prayer one night, so a man said: The prayer, the prayer. He said: May you lose your mother, do you teach us about prayer, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes combine the two in Medina."
"906","972 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Nafi narrated to me that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, hastened his travel one night, and he had been called urgently for one of his family, the daughter of Abu Ubayd, so he traveled until the twilight was about to disappear, while his companions called him to prayer, but he refused, until when they pressed him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combining these two prayers, Maghrib and Isha, and I am combining them."
"907","973 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when travel hurried him, would combine Maghrib and Isha."
"908","974 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine Maghrib and Isha when travel became serious."
"909","975 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ismail ibn Abi Dhuayb, he said: I was with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and when the sun set, we feared to say to him: The prayer. So he traveled until the darkness of Isha went and we saw the whiteness of the horizon, then he dismounted and prayed Maghrib three units and Isha two units, then he said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"910","976 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma, Ibn Abi Dawud, and Imran ibn Musa al-Tai narrated to us, they said: al-Rabi ibn Yahya al-Ushnani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Zuhr and Asr and Maghrib and Isha in Medina as a concession, without fear or illness."
"911","977 - Ali ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the sun set for him at Mecca, so he combined the two at Sarif, meaning the prayer."
"912","978 - Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hafs ibn Ubaydullah, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine Maghrib and Isha while traveling. Abu Jafar said: A group held that Zuhr and Asr share one time. They said: For that reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined them in the time of one of them, and likewise Maghrib and Isha in their view share a time, neither one missed until the time of the other ends. Others differed with them and said: Rather each of these prayers has a time distinct from the other. They said: As for what you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his combining two prayers, it is narrated as you mentioned. But there is no proof in that that he combined them in the time of one of them; it is possible his combining was as you said, and possible he prayed each in its own time, as Jabir ibn Zayd thought, who narrated that from Ibn Abbas, and Amr ibn Dinar after him. The people of the first view said: We found in some reports what indicates the description of the combining he did was as we said. So they mentioned about that what:"
"913","979 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, was urgently called for Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd while he was in Mecca, so he set out toward Medina, and traveled until the sun set and the stars appeared. A man accompanying him kept saying: The prayer, the prayer. And Salim said to him: The prayer. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when travel hurried him on a journey, would combine these two prayers, and I want to combine them. So he traveled until the twilight disappeared, then dismounted and combined them."
"914","980 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abdullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that when travel became serious he would combine Maghrib and Isha after the twilight disappeared, and would say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when travel became serious, would combine them. They said: This proves the description of his combining, how it was. The counter-argument from their opponent was that in the hadith of Ayyub in which he said: he traveled until the twilight disappeared then dismounted - none of the companions of Nafi mentioned that, neither Ubaydullah, nor Malik, nor al-Layth, nor those from whom we narrated the hadith of Ibn Umar in this chapter. He only reported that as part of the act of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and reported the combining from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without mentioning how he combined. As for the hadith of Ubaydullah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined them, then he mentioned the combining of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, how it was, and that it was after the twilight disappeared. It is possible he meant that his praying the last Isha, by which he was combining, was after the twilight disappeared, even though he had prayed Maghrib before the twilight disappeared, for he was never combining until he prayed the last Isha, by which he became one combining Maghrib and Isha. And others besides Ayyub narrated that explained as we said."
"915","981 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Usama ibn Zayd, he said: Nafi informed me that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was hurried by travel, so he traveled a journey, not dismounting except for Zuhr or Asr, and he delayed Maghrib until Salim called to him: The prayer. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, remained silent until near the disappearance of the twilight, then dismounted and combined them, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do thus when travel became serious. This hadith shows his dismounting for Maghrib was before the twilight disappeared, so the saying of Nafi: after the twilight disappeared, in the hadith of Ayyub, may have meant near the disappearance of the twilight, so that what was narrated from him does not contradict. And others besides Usama narrated this hadith from Nafi as Usama narrated it."
"916","982 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jabir narrated to me, he said: Nafi narrated to me, he said: I went out with Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, while he was heading to his land. We stopped at a stop, then a man came to him and said to him: Safiyya bint Abi Ubayd is dying, and I do not think you will reach her. So he left quickly with a man of Quraysh, and we traveled until the sun set and he did not pray the prayer, though my knowledge of my companion was that he guarded prayer. When he delayed I said: The prayer, may Allah have mercy on you. He turned to me and continued as he was, until when it was at the end of the twilight, he dismounted and prayed Maghrib then Isha after it had disappeared, then he turned to us and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when a matter hurried him, would do thus."
"917","983 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: al-Attaf ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from Nafi, he said: We came with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, until when we were on part of the road, he was urgently called for his wife, the daughter of Abu Ubayd, so he traveled quickly until the sun set, and prayer was announced but he did not dismount, until evening came and we thought he had forgotten. So I said: The prayer. He was silent until the twilight was about to disappear, then he dismounted and prayed Maghrib, and the twilight disappeared so he prayed Isha, and said: Thus we used to do with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when travel became serious. All of these narrate from Nafi that the dismounting of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, was before the twilight disappeared. We mentioned the possibility in the saying of Ayyub from Nafi: until the twilight disappeared, that it may mean near its disappearance, so the most proper for us is to take all these narrations as agreeing, not contradicting. So we take what was narrated from Ibn Umar that his dismounting for Maghrib was after the twilight disappeared as meaning near its disappearance, since it was narrated from him that that dismounting was before its disappearance. Even if they contradicted, the hadith of Ibn Jabir would be worthier, for the hadith of Ayyub also states that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would combine, then mentioned the act of Ibn Umar how it was. In the hadith of Ibn Jabir is the description of the combining of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), how it was, so it is worthier. If they say: There has been narrated from Anas what explained the combining how it was, so they mentioned about that what:"
"918","984 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jabir ibn Ismail informed me, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, the same. Meaning that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when travel hurried him by day, would combine Zuhr and Asr, and when he intended travel by night, would combine Maghrib and Isha, delaying Zuhr to the beginning of the time of Asr, combining them, and delaying Maghrib until combining it with Isha, until the twilight disappears. They said: This hadith shows he prayed Zuhr and Asr in the time of Asr, and that his combining was thus. The counter-argument against them from the people of the first view was that this hadith may bear what they mentioned. And it may be that the description of combining is from the words of al-Zuhri, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for he often did this, joining the hadith with his own words until it is thought to be part of the hadith. And it may be that his saying: to the beginning of the time of Asr, means to the nearest beginning of the time of Asr. If its meaning is one of what we directed it to, by which it is not necessary that he prayed it in the time of Asr, then there is no proof in this hadith for one who says he prayed it in the time of Asr. And if the basis of the hadith is that he prayed it in the time of Asr, and that was his combining, then Abdullah ibn Umar contradicted him in what we narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, also contradicted him."
"919","985 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: al-Muafa ibn Imran narrated to us, from Mughira ibn Ziyad al-Mawsili, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while traveling would delay Zuhr and hasten Asr, and delay Maghrib and hasten Isha. Then this Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them both, also - we narrated from him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would combine two prayers while traveling. Then there has been narrated from him what:"
"920","986 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Qabisa ibn Uqba and al-Firyabi narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umara ibn Umayr, from Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, he said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray any prayer outside its time except that he combined the two prayers at Muzdalifa and prayed Fajr that day outside its appointed time. So it is established by what we mentioned that what he witnessed of the combining of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is contrary to what our opponent interpreted. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports narrated about the combining of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It was mentioned therein that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined two prayers in residence without fear, as he combined them while traveling. So is it allowed for anyone in residence, not in fear nor illness, to delay Zuhr until near the changing of the sun then pray. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about negligence in prayer what:"
"921","987 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughira narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatada, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no negligence in sleep; negligence is only in wakefulness, by delaying a prayer until the time of another. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that delaying prayer until the time of the next is negligence. He said that while traveling, which shows he meant thereby the traveler and the resident. Since one who delays prayer until the time of the next is negligent, it is impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined two prayers in the manner that would make him negligent. Rather he combined them otherwise, praying each in its own time. And this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him - it is narrated from him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he combined two prayers, then he said what:"
"922","988 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Layth, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: No prayer is missed until the time of the next comes. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed that the coming of the time of the prayer after the preceding prayer is missing it. So it is established that what he knew of the combining of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was other than praying one of them in the time of the other. And Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, also said the same."
"923","989 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Qays and Sharik narrated to us, that they heard Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab say: Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked: What is negligence in prayer? He said: To delay it until the time of the next comes. They said: What was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when asked about prayer times - that he prayed Asr on the first day when the shadow of everything became equal to it, then prayed Zuhr on the second day at that very time - also indicates that, showing it is a time for both. It was said to them: There is no proof in this necessitating what you mentioned, for it may mean he prayed Zuhr on the second day near the time in which he prayed Asr on the first day. We mentioned that and the argument therein in the chapter on prayer times. The proof of that is his saying, upon him be peace: The time is between these two times. Had it been as our opponent says, there would be no time between them, since all before and after would be one time, and that would not prove each prayer has a time distinct from the other prayers. Another proof is that Abdullah ibn Abbas and Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about prayer times, then both said about negligence in prayer that it is leaving it until the time of the next enters. So it is established that the time of each prayer is distinct from the time of the next prayer. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by reasoning, we saw they agreed that Fajr should not be brought before its time nor delayed beyond it, for its time is خاص to it alone. By analogy, all prayers should be likewise, each distinct to its time, neither delayed beyond nor brought before it. If one objects with the prayer at Arafat and Muzdalifa, it is said: We saw they agreed that if the imam at Arafat prayed Zuhr in its usual time and Asr in its usual time, and did the same with Maghrib and Isha at Muzdalifa, praying each in its usual time as on other days, he would be doing wrong. Had he done that as resident or traveler elsewhere than Arafat and Muzdalifa, he would not be wrong. So it is established that Arafat and Muzdalifa are specified with this ruling, and the ruling of all else is contrary to them. So it is established by what we mentioned that what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of combining two prayers means delaying the first and hastening the second. And likewise the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him would combine them."
"924","990 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythama narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Uthman, he said: I came with Sad ibn Malik, hurrying for Hajj, and we would combine Zuhr and Asr, bringing forward part of this and delaying part of that, and combine Maghrib and Isha, bringing forward part of this and delaying part of that, until we reached Mecca."
"925","991 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid say: I accompanied Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, on a Hajj, and he would delay Zuhr and hasten Asr, and delay Maghrib and hasten Isha, and brighten the morning prayer. And all we held in this chapter about how to combine two prayers is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Most High have mercy on them."
"926","9 - Chapter on the middle prayer, which prayer is it? 992 - Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zibriqan, he said: A group of Quraysh gathered, and Zayd ibn Thabit passed them, so they sent two of their boys to ask him about the middle prayer. He said: It is Zuhr. Two men of them stood to him, and he said: It is Zuhr; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Zuhr at midday heat, and none would be behind him except one or two rows, while the people were in their midday rest and trade. So Allah Most High revealed: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Men will stop, or I will burn their houses."
"927","993 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Hakim, from al-Zibriqan, from Urwa, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Zuhr at midday heat, or he said at midday, and it was the heaviest prayer upon his Companions, so it was revealed: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, because before it are two prayers and after it two prayers."
"928","994 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Amr ibn Sulayman, from Abdur Rahman ibn Aban ibn Uthman, from his father, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: It is Zuhr."
"929","995 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn Umar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, the same."
"930","996 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ibn al-Yarbu al-Makhzumi, that he heard Zayd ibn Thabit say that."
"931","997 - Ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Haywa and Ibn Lahia, they said: Abu Sakhr informed us that he heard Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Qusayt say: I heard Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit say: I heard my father say that."
"932","998 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Rabia narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid al-Madani, from Abdur Rahman ibn Aflah, that some of his companions sent him to Abdullah ibn Umar to ask him about the middle prayer. He said: Convey peace to them and inform them that we used to say it is the one after the forenoon. He said: So they sent me back to him a second time, and I said: They convey peace to you and say: Clarify for us which prayer it is? He said: Convey peace to them and inform them that we used to say it is the prayer in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was turned toward the Kaba. He said: And we knew it, it is Zuhr. Abu Jafar said: A group went to what we mentioned and said: It is Zuhr, and they argued with what Zayd ibn Thabit argued, as we mentioned from him in the hadith of Rabi al-Muadhdhin, and with what we narrated about that from Ibn Umar. Others differed with them and said: As for the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit, there is nothing in it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except his saying: People will stop or I will burn their houses over them, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray Zuhr at midday heat, and only one or two rows would gather with him, so Allah Most High revealed this verse. So he inferred from that that it is Zuhr; this is a saying of Zayd ibn Thabit, and he did not narrate it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in this verse - in our view - there is no proof of that, for it is possible this verse was revealed to guard all prayers, the middle and others. So Zuhr was among what was intended, though it is not the middle. So by this verse guarding all prayers became obligatory, and among guarding them is attending them where they are prayed. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them about the prayer in whose attendance they were negligent: People will stop or I will burn their houses over them, meaning: people will stop neglecting this prayer which Allah Mighty and Majestic ordered them to guard, or I will burn their houses over them. And in none of that is proof of which prayer the middle prayer is. And a group said: This saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not for Zuhr prayer but for Friday prayer."
"933","999 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said to people lagging behind Friday: I intended to order a man to lead the people, then burn houses over people lagging behind Friday. So this Ibn Masud informs that that saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was for those lagging behind Friday in their houses. And he did not infer from that that Friday is the middle prayer; rather he said the opposite, that it is Asr, and we will bring that in its place, Allah Most High willing. And others of the Followers agreed with Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, on what he said."
"934","1000 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Humayd and others claimed, from al-Hasan, he said: The prayer which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to burn houses over its people was Friday prayer. And contrary to that has also been narrated from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him."
"935","1001 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, I intended to order a man with firewood to gather it, then order prayer to be announced, then order a man to lead the people, then go to men and burn their houses over them. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, had one of them known he would find a fat bone or two good trotters, he would attend Isha."
"936","1002 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad and Malik informed me, from Abu al-Zinad, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"937","1003 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to me, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: No prayer is heavier upon the hypocrites than Fajr and Isha. Had they known what is in them, they would come to them even crawling. I intended to order the caller to give the iqama, then order a man to lead the people, then take flames of fire and burn the house over whoever did not go out to prayer."
"938","1004 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdala informed us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he delayed the last Isha until a third of the night or near it, then he came while people were drowsy and bare, so he became severely angry, then said: Had a man invited people to a bone or two trotters, they would answer him, while they lag behind this prayer! I intended to order a man to lead the people, then stay behind against the people of these houses who lag behind this prayer and set them ablaze with fire."
"939","1005 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, from Asim, and he mentioned the same with its chain. So this Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, informs that the prayer about which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said this saying is Isha, and that did not indicate to him that it is the middle prayer. Rather contrary to that has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which we will mention in its place, Allah Most High willing. And Said ibn al-Musayyib among the Followers agreed with Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, on what he said."
"940","1006 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ata al-Khurasani informed us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The prayer which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to burn houses over whoever lagged behind it was the last Isha. And contrary to all that has been narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, that that saying was not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about prayer, but about another matter."
"941","1007 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Lahia narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: I asked Jabir: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Were it not for something, I would order a man to lead the people, then burn houses over what is in them. Jabir said: He only said that because of a man about whom something reached him, so he said: If he does not stop, I will burn his house over what is in it. So this Jabir informs that that saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was for lagging behind what should not be lagged behind. So neither in this nor in anything before it is proof of what the middle prayer is. Since it was negated by what we mentioned that there is any proof in what we narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit, we returned to what was narrated from Ibn Umar, and there is no report from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it; it is only from his saying, for he said: It is the prayer in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was turned toward the Kaba. And contrary to that has been narrated from him through another chain."
"942","1009 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me. 1010 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: The middle prayer is Asr prayer. Since what was narrated about that from Ibn Umar contradicted, this shows he had nothing therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we returned to what was narrated from others."
"943","1011 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Awf, from Abu Raja, he said: I prayed dawn behind Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he recited qunut before bowing, and said: This is the middle prayer."
"944","1012 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Qurra narrated to us, he said: Abu Raja narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: It is the dawn prayer."
"945","1013 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, from Hammam, from Qatada, from Abu al-Khalil, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the same."
"946","1014 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the same."
"947","1015 - Abu Bakra narrated to me, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliya, he said: I prayed dawn behind Abu Musa al-Ashari, and a man beside me among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the middle prayer. What Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, held of this was the saying of Allah Mighty and Majestic: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and stand devoutly for Allah. That qunut in his view was the qunut of dawn, so he thereby made the middle prayer the prayer in which qunut is performed in his view. And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, was opposed about this verse, concerning what it was revealed."
"948","1016 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from al-Harith ibn Shubayl, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: We used to speak in prayer until it was revealed: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and stand devoutly for Allah, so we were ordered to be silent."
"949","1017 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, and he mentioned the same."
"950","1018 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sufyan about this verse: And stand devoutly for Allah. He mentioned from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: They used to speak in prayer until this verse was revealed; so qunut is silence, and qunut is obedience."
"951","1019 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid about this verse: And stand devoutly for Allah. He said: Part of qunut is bowing, prostration, lowering the wing, and lowering the gaze out of awe of Allah."
"952","1020 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talha narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Amir al-Shabi, he said: Had qunut been as you say, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have had none of it; qunut is obedience, meaning: And whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger among you."
"953","1021 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Zayd about qunut, and he said: The whole prayer is qunut; as for what you do, I do not know what it is. So this Zayd ibn Arqam and those we mentioned with him inform that that qunut commanded in this verse is silence from the speech they used to speak in prayer. That excludes that this verse proves the qunut mentioned therein is the qunut performed in dawn prayer. A group denied that Ibn Abbas would recite qunut in dawn prayer, though we narrated that with its chain in the chapter on qunut in dawn prayer. Had this qunut mentioned in this verse been the qunut in dawn prayer, he would not have left it, since the Book commanded it. And it is narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that what he held therein is another meaning."
"954","1022 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Khidash al-Muhallabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The middle prayer is dawn, a division between the blackness of night and the whiteness of day. So this Ibn Abbas informed in this hadith that what made dawn prayer thereby the middle prayer is this reason. It is also possible that the saying of Allah Mighty and Majestic: And stand devoutly for Allah, meant thereby in dawn prayer, so that qunut would be length of standing, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when asked: Which prayer is most virtuous? He said: Length of qunut. We mentioned that with its chain in its place in this book of ours. And it is narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, also that she said: Dawn was kept as two units because of length of recitation in them. We mentioned that also elsewhere. And it is possible His saying: And stand devoutly for Allah, meant thereby in all prayers, the middle and others. And it is narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about the middle prayer that it is Asr."
"955","1023 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Razin ibn Ubaydullah al-Abdi, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: The middle prayer is Asr prayer, and stand devoutly for Allah. Since Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was differed from therein, we wanted to look into what was narrated from others. Those who held it is other than Asr also argued that there is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that. So they mentioned what:"
"956","1024 - Ali ibn Mabad ibn Nuh narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali and Nafi, freedman of Abdullah ibn Umar, narrated to me that Amr ibn Rafi, freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to them that he used to write copies of the Quran in the time of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, daughter of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), had me write a copy, and said to me: When you reach this verse of Surat al-Baqara, do not write it until you come to me so I dictate it to you as I memorized it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: When I reached it, I brought her the sheet I was writing, and she said: Write: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer."
"957","1025 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to me that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Amr ibn Rafi, the same, from Hafsa, except she did not mention the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"958","1026 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Yunus, freedman of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that he said: Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, ordered me, then he mentioned similar to the hadith of Hafsa, from the hadith of Ali ibn Mabad."
"959","1027 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abdur Rahman informed me, from his mother Umm Humayd bint Abdur Rahman, she asked Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, about the saying of Allah Mighty and Majestic: And the middle prayer. She said: We used to recite it in the first wording, in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer and stand devoutly for Allah. They said: Since Allah Mighty and Majestic said in these reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer, it is established that the middle is other than Asr. In our view there is no proof in that of what they mentioned; for Asr may be named Asr and also named the middle, so He mentioned it here by both names together. This is possible had what is in those reports of extra recitation beyond the recitation established by proof been ثابت, though the recitation established by proof repels all that contradicts it. And it is narrated that what was in the copy of Hafsa thereof is other than what we narrated in the first reports."
"960","1028 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salama, from Amr ibn Rafi, he said: It was written in the copy of Hafsa bint Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer, and it is Asr prayer. So this establishes what we directed the interpretation of the first reports to, of His saying: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer, that He named Asr prayer by Asr and by the middle. So the saying of one who held it is Asr prayer is established. And there is narrated from al-Bara ibn Azib therein what indicates abrogation of what was narrated therein from Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, and Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, and Umm Kulthum."
"961","1029 - Abu Shurayh, Muhammad ibn Zakariya ibn Yahya, narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Shaqiq ibn Uqba narrated to us, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: Guard the prayers and the Asr prayer was revealed, and we recited it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as long as Allah willed, then Allah Mighty and Majestic abrogated it and revealed: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer. So al-Bara ibn Azib informs in this hadith that the first recitation is what Aisha and Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated, and that the recitation established by proof abrogated it. If His second saying: And the middle prayer, abrogates Asr being the middle, then that abrogates it. And if it abrogates the recitation of one of its two names while affirming its other name, then it is established that the middle prayer is Asr prayer. Since this bears what we mentioned, we returned to what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"962","1030 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Zaida ibn Qudama narrated to us, he said: I heard Asim narrating from Zirr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We fought the Confederates and they distracted us from Asr prayer until the sun was about to set. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, fill the hearts of those who distracted us from the middle prayer with fire, fill their houses with fire, and fill their graves with fire. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We used to think it was Fajr prayer. So this Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, informed that they used to think before this saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it was dawn, until they heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say this that day, so they knew thereby that it is Asr."
"963","1031 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Shuba, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he sat on the Day of the Trench on an opening of the trench, then he mentioned similar, except he did not mention the saying of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: We used to think it was dawn."
"964","1032 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I said to Abida: Ask Ali for us about the middle prayer. So he asked him, and he mentioned similar and added: We used to think it was Fajr, until I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say this."
"965","1033 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talha narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Murra, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Except he did not mention the saying of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: We used to think it was Fajr."
"966","1034 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Talha, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"967","1035 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on a campaign, and did not return from it until evening kept him from Asr prayer beyond the time in which he used to pray it, then he mentioned the same."
"968","1036 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sadwayh narrated to us, from Abbad, from Hilal, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"969","1037 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam and Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said on the Day of the Trench, then he mentioned the same. So this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informs from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is Asr prayer; so how can his opinion be accepted when it contradicts that."
"970","1038 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Sadaqa ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Dihqan narrated to me, he said: Khalid Sabalan informed me, from Kuhayl ibn Harmala al-Namari, from Abu Hurayra, that he came until he stopped at Damascus with the family of Abu Kulthum al-Dumi, then he went to the mosque and sat in its western part, and they discussed the middle prayer and differed therein. He said: We differed therein as you differed, while we were at the courtyard of the house of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and among us was the righteous man Abu Hashim ibn Utba ibn Rabia ibn Abd Shams. He said: I will learn that for you. So he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was bold with him, so he asked permission and entered, then came out to us and informed us that it is Asr prayer."
"971","1039 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Janab narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd, from Musa ibn Wardan, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The middle prayer is Asr prayer."
"972","1040 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada. 1041 - And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Aruba narrated to us, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Samura, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. These are reports that came successively and soundly from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the middle prayer is Asr. A number of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also said that."
"973","1042 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Ubayy ibn Kab, he said: The middle prayer is Asr prayer."
"974","1043 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, from Hammam, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, the same."
"975","1044 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abi Ubada narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the same."
"976","1045 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Abdur Rahman ibn Labiba al-Taifi, that he asked Abu Hurayra about the middle prayer. He said: I will recite the Quran to you until you know it. Does not Allah Mighty and Majestic say in His Book: Establish prayer from the decline of the sun - Zuhr; until the darkness of the night - Maghrib; and after the Isha prayer, three times of privacy for you - the night prayer; and He says: The recitation of dawn was witnessed - dawn. Then He said: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and stand devoutly for Allah - it is Asr, it is Asr. If one says: Why was the middle prayer named Asr prayer? It is said: The people said two sayings: A group said it was named so because it is between two night prayers and two day prayers. And others said about that what:"
"977","1046 - al-Qasim ibn Jafar narrated to me, he said: I heard Bahr ibn al-Hakam al-Kaysani say: I heard Abu Abdur Rahman Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aisha say: When Adam, upon him be peace, was forgiven at dawn, he prayed two units, so it became dawn; and Ishaq was ransomed at Zuhr, so Ibrahim, upon him be peace, prayed four, so it became Zuhr; and Uzayr was raised, and it was said to him: How long did you remain? He said: A day, then he saw the sun and said: Or part of a day, so he prayed four units, so it became Asr. And it was said: Uzayr, upon him be peace, was forgiven, and Dawud, upon him be peace, was forgiven at Maghrib, so he stood and prayed four units, then struggled and sat in the third, so Maghrib became three. And the first who prayed the last Isha was our Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). For that they said: The middle prayer is Asr prayer. This - in our view - is a sound meaning, for if the first of prayers is dawn and the last is the last Isha, then the middle between the first and last is Asr. For that we said: The middle prayer is Asr prayer, and this is the saying of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Most High have mercy on them."
"978","10 - Chapter on the time in which Fajr is prayed, what time is it? 1047 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We were believing women praying dawn with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), wrapped in their cloaks, then they would return to their families, and no one would recognize them."
"979","1048 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned the same."
"980","1049 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, the same. Except he said: And they would not recognize one another due to darkness."
"981","1050 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amra bint Abdur Rahman, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, similar. Except he said: And they would not be recognized due to darkness."
"982","1051 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Usama ibn Zayd, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Bashir ibn Abi Masud informed me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed dawn darkly, then prayed it brightly, then did not return to brightening until Allah Mighty and Majestic took him."
"983","1052 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me. 1053 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Nuhayk ibn Yarim narrated to me, from Mughith ibn Sumayy, that he said: I prayed dawn with Ibn al-Zubayr in darkness, then I turned to Abdullah ibn Umar and said: What is this? He said: This is our prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with Abu Bakr, and with Umar. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, brightened it."
"984","1054 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas ibn Malik, from Zayd ibn Thabit, they said: We ate suhur with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we went out to prayer. I said: How much between that? He said: As much as a man recites fifty verses."
"985","1055 - Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Mansur ibn Zadhdhan, from Qatada, from Anas, from Zayd ibn Thabit, the same."
"986","1056 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hasan say: When al-Hajjaj came, he began delaying prayer, so we asked Jabir ibn Abdullah about that. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray dawn - or he said: they used to pray dawn - in darkness."
"987","1057 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hasan, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: They used to pray dawn in darkness."
"988","1058 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Hassan al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: My two grandmothers Safiyya bint Ulayba and Duhayba bint Ulayba narrated to me that Qayla bint Makhrama informed them that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was praying Fajr with his Companions, and it was established when dawn split and the stars were interwoven in the sky, and men could hardly recognize one another in the darkness."
"989","1059 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubada and al-Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us, they said: Qurra ibn Khalid al-Sadusi narrated to us, he said: Dirghama ibn Ulayba ibn Harmala al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me from my grandfather, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a party of the tribe, and he prayed with us the morning prayer, then finished, and I could hardly recognize the faces of the people, as if in darkness."
"990","1060 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Qurra narrated to us, from Dirghama ibn Ulayba, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Jafar said: A group went to these reports and said: Thus one does in Fajr prayer, darkening it, for it is more virtuous than brightening it. Others differed with them and said: Rather brightening it is more virtuous than darkening. They argued therein with what:"
"991","1061 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid say: Abdullah performed Hajj, and Alqama ordered me to stay with him. When it was the night of Muzdalifa and dawn rose, he said: Give the iqama. I said: O Abu Abdur Rahman, at this hour I never saw you pray. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray - meaning this prayer - except at this hour in this place on this day. Abdullah said: They are two prayers moved from their times: Maghrib prayer after people come to Muzdalifa, and dawn prayer when dawn breaks. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that."
"992","1062 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: I went out with Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, to Mecca, and he prayed Fajr on the Day of Sacrifice when dawn shone, then he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These two prayers are moved from their times in this place: Maghrib and Fajr prayer at this hour."
"993","1063 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, he said: Zakariya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sumayra, he said: Abu Tarif narrated to me that he was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the fortress of Taif, and he would pray Fajr with us until if a man shot his arrow he could see the places of his arrows."
"994","1064 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would delay Fajr like its name."
"995","1065 - Abu Bakra and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salama, he said: I entered with my father upon Abu Barza, and my father asked him about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: He would finish dawn prayer while a man could recognize the face of his companion, and he would recite in it from sixty to a hundred verses. They said: These reports indicate the delaying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his brightening it. In the hadith of Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he would pray dawn on other days at a different time than he prayed at Muzdalifa, and that this prayer is moved from its time. Abu Jafar said: There is nothing in any of these reports nor in what preceded them proving which is most virtuous; for he may have done something while another is more virtuous, as a concession to his nation, as he performed ablution once each, though his ablution three times each was more virtuous. So we wanted to look into what was narrated from him besides these reports, whether therein is what proves virtue in any of that."
"996","1066 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Brighten Fajr, for whenever you brighten it, it is greater in reward, and he said: for your rewards."
"997","1067 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysara narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from men of his people of the Ansar, Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray dawn in daylight; whenever you do it in daylight, it is greater in reward."
"998","1068 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Illuminate with Fajr, for it is greater in reward."
"999","1069 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Asim ibn Umar, from men of his people of the Ansar, Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray dawn in daylight; whenever you do it in daylight, it is greater in reward."
"1000","1070 - Bakr ibn Idris ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Dawud, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Asim ibn Umar, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi ibn Khadij, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Illuminate with Fajr, for it is greater in reward."
"1001","1071 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shababa ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sayyar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, from Bilal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Abu Jafar said: These reports inform about where virtue lies, that it is brightening Fajr. The first reports in the first two sections inform about the time in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray, what time it was. It is possible he would darken at times and brighten at times as a concession. The most virtuous thereof is what he clarified in the hadith of Rafi, so that the reports do not contradict. This is the aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter."
"1002","1072 - As for what was narrated from those after him therein, Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Mansur ibn al-Mutamir narrating from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Qurra, from Hibban ibn al-Harith, he said: We ate suhur with Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and when he finished suhur, he ordered the caller, who gave the iqama. Abu Jafar said: This hadith shows Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, entered prayer at dawn rise, but there is no proof therein of the time of his finishing, what time it was. It is possible he lengthened recitation therein, attaining both darkness and brightness, and that in our view is good. So we wanted to see whether anything was narrated from him indicating any of that."
"1003","1073 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi, from his father, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, would pray Fajr with us while we watched the sun, fearing it had risen. This hadith informs that his finishing was in a state of brightness, which indicates what we mentioned. And there is also narrated from him therein the command to brighten."
"1004","1074 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Said ibn Ubayd, from Ali ibn Rabia, he said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, say: O Qanbar, brighten, brighten."
"1005","1075 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sayf ibn Harun al-Burjumi informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Sala al-Hamdani, from Abd Khayr, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, would brighten Fajr at times and darken it at times. So his darkening may have been a darkening by which he attains brightening. And similar has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1006","1076 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash informed us, from Abu Husayn, from Kharasha ibn al-Hurr, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, would brighten Fajr and darken, praying between that, reciting Surat Yusuf and Yunus, short oft-repeated chapters and the Mufassal. Successive reports are narrated from him indicating he would finish his prayer brightened."
"1007","1077 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, that he heard Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabia say: We prayed dawn behind Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he recited in it Surat Yusuf and Surat al-Hajj in slow recitation. I said: By Allah, then he must have stood when dawn rose? He said: Yes."
"1008","1078 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Saib ibn Yazid say: I prayed dawn behind Umar, and he recited al-Baqara in it. When they finished, they looked toward the sun and said: It has risen. He said: Had it risen, it would not have found us heedless."
"1009","1079 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed dawn with us and recited Bani Israil and al-Kahf until I began looking at the walls of the mosque whether the sun had risen."
"1010","1080 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara informed me, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, recited in dawn prayer al-Kahf and Bani Israil."
"1011","1081 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, recited in dawn Surat al-Kahf and Surat Yusuf."
"1012","1082 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Budayl ibn Maysara narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: al-Ahnaf ibn Qays prayed dawn with us at Aqul al-Kufa, reciting al-Kahf in the first unit and Surat Yusuf in the second. He said: And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed dawn with us, reciting them in both."
"1013","1083 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Murra, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed Fajr with us in Mecca, reciting Yusuf in the first unit until he reached: And his eyes whitened with grief, and he was suppressed, then he bowed, then stood and recited al-Najm in the second unit and prostrated, then stood and recited: When the earth is shaken with its shaking, raising his voice in recitation until had anyone been in the valley he would have made him hear."
"1014","1084 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, that he prayed Fajr with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he recited Yusuf in the first unit and al-Najm in the second, and prostrated."
"1015","1085 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Amash narrating, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Husayn ibn Sabra, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed with us, and he mentioned the same. Abu Jafar said: Since what we mentioned was narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in the hadith of Abdullah ibn Amir that that recitation was slow recitation, we hold - and Allah knows best - that his entering it was only in darkness, and his finishing it was only after it had brightened intensely. And thus he used to write to his governors."
"1016","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from al-Muhajir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Abu Musa: pray Fajr in darkness - or he said: at ghalas (deep darkness) - and lengthen the recitation."
"1017","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad, from al-Muhajir, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the same. Do you not see that he commands them to enter it at ghalas and to lengthen the recitation. So likewise in our view: he intended them to reach the isfar (daylight brightness), and likewise everyone besides Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from whom we narrated anything on this matter had also held this same position."
"1018","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in Fajr prayer and recited Surah Al Imran, so they said: the sun has almost risen, so he said: had it risen it would not have found us heedless."
"1019","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in Fajr prayer and recited Surah al-Baqarah in both rakahs together, then when he finished Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: the sun almost rose, so he said: had it risen it would not have found us heedless. Abu Jafar said: So here is Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him: he entered it at a time other than isfar, then he prolonged the recitation in it until sunrise was feared for him. And this was in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), close to their time with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and to his practice, with none of them objecting to him - so that is proof of their concurrence with him. Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did the same after him, and none of those present among them objected to him. So it is established thereby that this is how Fajr prayer is done, and that what they knew of the practice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) does not contradict that. If one says: then what is the meaning of Ibn Umar's statement to Mughith ibn Sumayy when he prayed Fajr at ghalas: this is our prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him; then when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed it at isfar - it is said to him: it may be that he meant thereby the time of entering it, not the time of exiting it, so that it agrees with what we narrated before, and his statement then Uthman prayed it at isfar means so that their exit would be at a time when they felt safe and did not fear being assassinated as Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was assassinated. And there has also been narrated from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, what indicates that he used to enter it in darkness due to his lengthening the recitation in it."
"1020","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Yahya ibn Said and Rabiah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that al-Furafisah ibn Umayr al-Hanafi informed him, he said: I learned Surah Yusuf only from Uthman ibn Affan's, may Allah be pleased with him, recitation of it in Fajr, due to how often he repeated it. So this also indicates that he used to follow the practice of those before him: entering it in darkness and exiting it at isfar. And Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to finish it at isfar."
"1021","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, he said: Ibrahim al-Taymi related to me, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, that he used to pray with their imam among Taym, who would recite to them a surah from the Mi'in (long surahs), then he would come to Abd Allah and find him in Fajr prayer."
"1022","Abu al-Darda Hashim ibn Muhammad al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: we used to pray with Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and he used to pray Fajr prayer at isfar. So we have understood thereby that Abd Allah used to pray at isfar, so we know thereby that his exit from it was at that time; and these reports do not mention his entering it at what time it was, so that in our view - and Allah knows best - is like what was narrated from others among his companions. And the like of this was also done in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1023","Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafii narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Irak ibn Malik saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I arrived in Madinah while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at Khaybar, and a man of Banu Ghifar was leading the people, so I heard him reciting in Fajr prayer in the first rakah Surah Maryam and in the second: Wayl lil-mutaffifin."
"1024","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Khuthaym ibn Irak, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, the same, except that he said: so he left Siba ibn Urfatah al-Ghifari in charge of Madinah, so I prayed behind him. So here is Siba ibn Urfatah: in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointing him, leading the people in Fajr prayer like this, lengthening the recitation in it until it covered both ghalas and isfar. And there has also been narrated from Abu al-Darda something of this."
"1025","Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, he said: Muawiyah led us in Fajr at ghalas, so Abu al-Darda said: pray this prayer at isfar, for it is more comprehending (afqah) for you; you only wish to be free for your needs. This in our view - and Allah Most High knows best - is from Abu al-Darda objecting to their abandoning the prolonging of recitation until the time of isfar, not objecting to their time of entering it. So since what we narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the isfar at which the departure from prayer occurs, along with what we narrated from them of lengthening the recitation in that prayer, it is established that praying Fajr at isfar ought not to be abandoned by anyone, and that ghalas is only done together with isfar: this at the beginning of the prayer and that at its end. If one says: then what is the meaning of what was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the women used to pray Fajr with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then leave unrecognizable due to ghalas - it is said to him: it may be that this was before the command to lengthen the recitation in it, for indeed:"
"1026","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: prayer was first obligated two rakahs two rakahs, then when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah he added to every prayer the like of it except Maghrib, for it is witr (odd), and Fajr prayer due to the length of its recitation; and when he traveled he returned to his first prayer. So Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed in this report that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray, before prayer was completed, in the manner he prays when traveling, and the traveler's rule is lightening the prayer; then afterwards it was consolidated: some prayers were increased and some were commanded to be lengthened. So it is possible - and Allah knows best - that what he did of praying it at ghalas, with the women leaving it unrecognizable due to ghalas, was at the time when he prayed it as he now prays while traveling; then he was commanded to lengthen the recitation in it, and that his practice in residence be unlike what he does while traveling, lengthening this and lightening that. And he said: pray Fajr at isfar - i.e. lengthen the recitation in it. That is not that they enter it at the end of the isfar time, but that they exit it at the isfar time. So thereby what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated is established as abrogated by what we mentioned, along with what the practice of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him also indicated, in their reaching isfar at the time of their departure from it and their agreement upon that. Until Ibrahim al-Nakhai said what:"
"1027","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never agreed upon anything as they agreed upon tanwir (praying Fajr at brightness). So he informed that they had agreed upon that, so in our view - and Allah knows best - their agreement upon other than what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done is not possible except after the abrogation of that and the establishment of its opposite. So what is proper: entering Fajr at the time of ghalas and exiting it at the time of isfar, in agreement with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions. And it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah Most High have mercy on them."
"1028","Chapter 11: the time in which it is recommended to pray Zuhr prayer. Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zibriqan, from Urwah, from Usamah ibn Zayd, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Zuhr at al-hajir (midday heat)."
"1029","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Sad ibn Ibrahim related to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hasan saying: we asked Jabir ibn Abd Allah and he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Zuhr at al-hajirah, or when the sun declined."
"1030","Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah al-Laythi narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Harith, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: we used to pray Zuhr with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I would take a handful of pebbles or soil and put it in my palm, then shift it to the other palm until it cooled, then place it at the spot of my forehead due to the severity of the heat."
"1031","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Wahb, from Khabbab, he said: we complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the heat of the sun-baked ground at midday, but he did not grant us relief."
"1032","Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Khaythamah, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Wahb, from Khabbab, the same. Abu Ishaq said: he used to hasten Zuhr, so the heat weighed heavily upon them."
"1033","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib or one like him among his companions, he said Khabbab said: we complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the heat of the sun-baked ground, but he did not grant us relief."
"1034","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq (h). And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym and Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abu Ishaq (h). And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah, from Khabbab, the same."
"1035","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us (h). And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I never saw anyone hasten Zuhr prayer more than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she excepted neither her father nor Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"1036","Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Awf al-Arabi narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, he said: I heard Abu Barzah saying: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray al-hajir which you call Zuhr when the sun had declined."
"1037","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Hamzah al-Aidhi, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped at a place, he would not depart from it until he had prayed Zuhr. A man said: even if it were midday? So he said: even if it were midday."
"1038","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out when the sun had declined and prayed Zuhr with them."
"1039","Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Mihran (h). And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja informed us, he said: Zaidah informed us, from Sulayman, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, he said: I prayed Zuhr behind Abd Allah ibn Masud when the sun had declined, and he said: this - by the One besides whom there is no god - is the time of this prayer. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted this and recommended hastening Zuhr in all seasons at the beginning of its time, and they argued for that with what we mentioned. Others differed with them and said: as for the winter days, it is hastened as you mentioned; but as for the summer days, it is delayed until it is cooled. And they argued for that with what:"
"1040","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muhajir Abu al-Hasan, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr, he said: we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at a stopping place, so Bilal called the adhan, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wait, O Bilal. Then he wanted to call it, so he said: Wait, O Bilal. Then he wanted to call it, so he said: Wait, O Bilal - until we saw the shadows of the hillocks. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell, so cool down the prayer when the heat is severe."
"1041","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: cool down the prayer, for the severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell; so cool down the prayer when the heat is severe."
"1042","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1043","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he informed him, from Abu Salamah and Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1044","Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1045","Ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth related to me, he said: Ibn al-Had related to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1046","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, from Abu Salamah, and from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1047","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1048","Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, used to relate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"1049","Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: my uncle narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Said and Salman al-Agharr, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When it is a hot day, cool down the prayer, for the severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell."
"1050","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and from Awf, from al-Hasan, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell, so cool down the prayer."
"1051","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ibrahim, from Yazid ibn Aws, from Thabit ibn Qays, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (h). And from Abu Zuraah, from Thabit ibn Qays, from Abu Musa, raising it, he said: Cool down Zuhr, for what you feel of heat is from an exhalation of Hell. So in these reports is the command to cool Zuhr due to severe heat, and that occurs only in summer. So that contradicts what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of hastening Zuhr in the heat, per the first reports we mentioned. If one says: then what proves one of the two commands is preferable? It is said to him: because it has been narrated that hastening Zuhr in the heat used to be done and was then abrogated."
"1052","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main and Tamim ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Zuhr at al-hajir, then said: The severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell, so cool down the prayer. So al-Mughirah informed in this report of his that the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to cool Zuhr came after he had prayed it in the heat. So thereby the hastening of Zuhr in severe heat is established as abrogated, and acting upon cooling in severe heat became obligatory. And it has been narrated from Anas ibn Malik and Abu Masud that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to hasten it in winter and delay it in summer."
"1053","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us with that, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth related to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib related to me, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Muhammad ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Bashir ibn Abi Masud informed me, from Abu Masud, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying Zuhr when the sun declined, and sometimes delaying it in severe heat."
"1054","And with its isnad, from Abu Masud, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastening it in winter and delaying it in summer."
"1055","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Harami ibn Umarah narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaldah related to me, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - when the cold was severe he prayed early, and when the heat was severe he cooled the prayer."
"1056","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Thabit narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaldah narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - when it was winter he hastened Zuhr, and when it was summer he cooled it. Abu Jafar said: So this is the Sunnah in our view for Zuhr prayer, per what Abu Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, mention of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer. And there is nothing in what we presented in the first section that requires contradicting any of this; for the reports of Usamah, Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, Khabbab, and Abu Barzah - all in our view are abrogated by the report of al-Mughirah which we narrated in the latter section. As for the report of Ibn Masud about Zuhr prayer when the sun declined and his swearing that is its time - that report does not say that was from him in summer, nor that it was from him in winter, and there is no indication therein against anything else. And this Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: al-Zuhri narrated from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr when the sun declined, then Abu Khaldah came and explained from him that he prayed it in winter hastened and in summer delayed - so it is possible that what Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated is likewise. Then if one arguing for hastening Zuhr cites what:"
"1057","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash informed us, from Abu Husayn, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: al-Hajjaj heard his call to Zuhr while in the cemetery, so he sent to him and said: What is this prayer? He said: I prayed with Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, when the sun declined. So he dismissed him and said: Do not call the adhan nor lead. It is said to him: there is nothing in this report that the time in which Suwayd saw them was in summer; and it is possible it was in winter, with summer's ruling in their view being otherwise."
"1058","And the proof of that is that Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that Umar said to Abu Mahdhurah at Makkah: You are in a hot land of severe heat, so cool down, then cool down the adhan for the prayer. Do you not see that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in this report commanded Abu Mahdhurah to cool down due to severe heat. And most fitting for us is to carry what Suwayd narrated from him as not contradicting that - that it was from him at a time without heat. Then if one says: the ruling of Zuhr is to hasten it in all seasons and not delay it, as narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the report of Khabbab, Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, Jabir, and Abu Barzah - and what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of commanding them to cool was a concession to them due to severe heat, because their mosque had no shade - and there is mentioned regarding that what was narrated from Maymun ibn Mihran:"
"1059","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Malih narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, he said: There is no harm in prayer at midday; they only disliked prayer at midday because they used to pray at Makkah which was severely hot, and they had no shade, so he said: Cool it. It is said to him: this reasoning is impossible; for had it been as you mentioned, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have delayed it while traveling - where there is no shelter nor shade - per the report of Abu Dharr, and would have prayed it at the beginning of its time without shelter or shade. So his abandoning prayer then is proof that what he did of commanding to cool was not so they would be in severe heat in shelter then come out and pray Zuhr when the heat subsides. For had that been so, he would have prayed it where there is no shelter at the beginning of its time. Rather what he did in this statement in our view - and Allah knows best - is obligating that this is its Sunnah, whether shelter exists or not. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad - may Allah Most High have mercy on them."
"1060","Chapter 12: Asr prayer - is it hastened or delayed? Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah al-Ansari, then al-Zafari, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I heard him saying: No one hastened Asr prayer more than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The two men of the Ansar farthest in house from the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mosque were Abu Lubabah ibn Abd al-Mundhir, brother of Banu Amr ibn Awf, and Abu Abs ibn Jabr of Banu Harithah: the house of Abu Lubabah at Quba and the house of Abu Abs among Banu Harithah. Yet they would pray Asr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then come to their people who had not prayed it - due to the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastening it."
"1061","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We used to pray Asr, then one would go out to Banu Amr ibn Awf and find them praying Asr."
"1062","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, he said: al-Zuhri and Ishaq ibn Abd Allah related to me, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr, then one going to Quba would go. One of them said: while they were praying; the other said: while the sun was elevated."
"1063","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas (h)."
"1064","And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas, he said: We used to pray Asr, then one going to Quba would go and reach them while the sun was elevated."
"1065","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr, then one going to al-Awali would go while the sun was elevated. Al-Zuhri said: al-Awali is at two and three miles, and I think he said: and four."
"1066","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr while the sun was elevated and alive, then one going to al-Awali would go and reach al-Awali while the sun was elevated."
"1067","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Zaidah informed us, from Mansur, from Ribi, he said: Abu al-Abyad narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lead us in Asr while the sun was white, then I would return to my people, while they were sitting on the outskirts of Madinah, and say to them: Stand and pray, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has prayed. So reports from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, differed in this report: what Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, Ishaq ibn Abd Allah, and Abu al-Abyad narrated from Anas indicates hastening it, for in their report the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it, then one going to the place they mentioned would find them not yet having prayed Asr. And we know those people only prayed it before the sun yellowed - so this is proof of hastening. As for what al-Zuhri narrated from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him - that we prayed it with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we would reach al-Awali while the sun was elevated - it is possible it was elevated yet yellowed. So this report of Anas has become conflicted, for the meaning of what al-Zuhri narrated of it differs from what Ishaq ibn Abd Allah, Asim ibn Umar, and Abu al-Abyad narrated from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. And there has also been narrated on that from others besides Anas. Of that is what:"
"1068","Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Abu Waqid al-Laythi narrated to us, he said: Abu Arwa narrated to us, he said: I used to pray Asr with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Madinah, then come to the tree at Dhu al-Hulayfah before the sun set - it being at two farsakhs. So in this report he would travel after Asr two farsakhs before the sun set. So it is possible that was traveling on foot, and possible it was traveling on camels and mounts."
"1069","So we examined that, and Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Salim al-Saigh narrated to us, he said: Mualla and Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, they said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abu Waqid, he said: Abu Arwa narrated to us, he said: I used to pray Asr with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then walk to Dhu al-Hulayfah, reaching them before the sun set. So in this report he would reach it walking. As for his statement before the sun set - it is possible that was when the sun had yellowed with only very little of it remaining. And there has been narrated from Abu Masud similar to that."
"1070","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib related to me, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Muhammad ibn Shihab, he said: I heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr saying: Bashir ibn Abi Masud informed me, from his father, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr while the sun was white and elevated; a man setting out from it to Dhu al-Hulayfah would travel six miles before sunset. So this report agreed with the report of Abu Arwa, adding that he prayed it while the sun was elevated - so that is proof he delayed it. And there has been narrated from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, also what indicates this, which is:"
"1071","Nassar ibn Harb al-Mismai al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ribi, from Abu al-Abyad, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr while the sun was white and high. So Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, informed in this report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed it while the sun was white and high - so that is proof he delayed it. Then between the time he prayed it and its setting would be the span a man traveled to Dhu al-Hulayfah and to the places mentioned in these reports. And there has been narrated from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, also on that, which is:"
"1072","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Sadaqah, freedman of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from Anas, that he was asked about prayer times, so he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr between these two prayers of yours. So that may mean by his statement between these two prayers of yours: between Zuhr and Maghrib - so that is proof of delaying Asr. And it may mean: between your hastening and your delaying - so that is also proof of delay, though not severe delay. So since that bears what we mentioned, and in the report of Abu al-Abyad from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it while the sun was white and high, it proves he delayed it. Then if one says: how is that so, when it has been narrated from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, censuring whoever delays Asr - so he mentioned regarding that what:"
"1073","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he said: I entered upon Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, after Zuhr, and he stood praying Asr. When he finished his prayer we mentioned hastening prayer - or he mentioned it - so he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: That is the prayer of the hypocrites - he said it thrice - one of them sits until when the sun yellows and is between the two horns of Satan, he stands and pecks four, remembering Allah in them only little. It is said to him: Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, clarified in this report what the disliked delay is: it is only the delay after which Asr can only be prayed pecked with little remembrance of Allah. As for a prayer one prays composed, remembering Allah Most High in it composed, before the sun changes - that is nothing of the former. And most fitting for us in these reports, since they came this way, is to carry them and construe their aspects upon agreement, not upon disagreement and contradiction. So we make the disliked delay therein what al-Ala clarified from Anas, and make the recommended time of it to pray in what Abu al-Abyad clarified from Anas, agreed upon by Abu Masud. Then if one says: there has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, what indicates hastening it - so he mentioned what:"
"1074","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, related to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr while the sun was in her room before it appeared."
"1075","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri - he heard Urwah relating from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr while the sun was in her room, the shade not yet extended."
"1076","Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr while the sun was shining in my room. It is said to him: it is possible that was so, while he had delayed Asr due to the shortness of her room - the sun would not leave it except close to its setting - so there is no proof in this report for hastening Asr. And he mentioned regarding that what:"
"1077","Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us (h). And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, he said: I entered with my father upon Abu Barzah, and he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray Asr, then a man would return to the farthest part of Madinah while the sun was alive. It is said to him: our answer to this has already passed in what preceded of this chapter. So we found nothing in these reports, when authenticated and gathered, indicating except delaying Asr; and we found nothing of them indicating hastening it except opposed by another. So we thereby preferred delaying Asr, except that it is prayed while the sun is white, at a time after which a span of its time remains before sunset. Had we been left to analogy, hastening all prayers at the beginnings of their times would be best; but following what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with reports successively transmitted, is preferable. And there has been narrated from his Companions after him what indicates that also."
"1078","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Nafi, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to his governors: The most important of your affairs to me is prayer; whoever guards it and maintains it guards his religion, and whoever wastes it is more wasting of what is besides it. Pray Asr while the sun is elevated, white and clear, for the span a rider travels two or three farsakhs."
"1079","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Hakim narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, he said: We were with Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, at a funeral, and he did not pray Asr, remaining silent until we reminded him repeatedly; he did not pray Asr until we saw the sun over the tallest mountain of Madinah."
"1080","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Those before you hastened Zuhr more and delayed Asr more than you. So here is Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, writing to his governors - they being the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - commanding them to pray Asr while the sun is white and elevated. Then Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, delayed it until Ikrimah saw it over the tallest mountain of Madinah. Then Ibrahim informs of those before him - meaning the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the companions of Abd Allah - that they delayed Asr more than those after them. So since this came from their actions and statements in agreement upon what we mentioned, and it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed it while the sun was elevated - and in some reports high - holding to these reports and abandoning their opposite became obligatory: that they delay Asr, provided its delay does not bring its delayer into the time of which Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, informed in the report of al-Ala that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the prayer of the hypocrites; for that time is the time to which delaying Asr is disliked. As for what precedes it of its time - in which the sun has not yellowed, and a man can pray Asr in it composed, remembering Allah in it composed, and exit prayer while the sun is likewise - there is no harm in delaying Asr to that time, and that is best due to what has been successively transmitted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions after him. And it has been narrated from Abu Qilabah that he said: Asr was only named so due to taasur - i.e. delay."
"1081","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to us with that, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid informed us, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Asr was only named Asr to be delayed (tu'sar). So Abu Qilabah informed that this name of it is only because its course is to be delayed. And this which we preferred - delaying Asr without that being to a time when the sun has changed or yellowness entered it - is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah Most High have mercy on them; and by it we take. Then if one arguing for hastening it also cites what:"
"1082","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Najashi related to me, he said: Rafi ibn Khadij related to me, he said: We used to pray Asr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then slaughter a camel, divide it into ten portions, then cook and eat ripe meat before the sun set. It is said to him: it is possible they did that with speed of work, though you delayed Asr - so in this report in our view there is no proof against whoever holds delaying Asr. And we mentioned in the chapter of prayer times, in the report of Buraydah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when asked about prayer times, prayed Asr on the first day while the sun was white, elevated and clear, then prayed it on the second day while the sun was elevated, delaying it beyond what he had delayed it on the first day. So he had delayed it on both days, not hastened it at the beginning of its time as he did with others. So thereby it is established that the time of Asr in which it ought to be prayed is what those who hold delaying it held, not what the others held. (End of the Book of Adhan and Times.)"
"1083","Chapter 13: raising the hands at the opening of prayer - to where with them? Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Said ibn Saman, freedman of the Zurqis, they said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon us and said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he stood for prayer, raised his hands extended. So some people adopted that: a man raises his hands when opening prayer extended, without specifying anything in that, arguing with this report. Others differed with them and said: rather he should raise his hands until parallel with his shoulders. And they argued for that with what:"
"1084","Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when he stood for the prescribed prayer he said Allahu Akbar and raised his hands parallel with his shoulders. And with what:"
"1085","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he opened prayer, raise his hands until parallel with his shoulders. And with what:"
"1086","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Ibn Shihab (h). And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with its isnad the same. And with what:"
"1087","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Jabir, he said: I saw Salim ibn Abd Allah, when he opened prayer, raise his hands parallel with his shoulders. So I asked him about that, and he said: I saw Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, doing that, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing that. And with what:"
"1088","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Humayd al-Saidi among ten of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them Abu Qatadah, he said: Abu Humayd said: I am the most knowledgeable of you of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer. They said: Why - by Allah you were neither the most following of him among us nor the earliest of us in his companionship? So he said: Yes indeed. They said: Then demonstrate. So he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opened prayer he raised his hands until parallel with his shoulders. He said: So they all said: You have spoken truthfully; this is how he prayed. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted this and said: the raising in the takbir at prayer's opening reaches the shoulders and does not pass them, arguing with these reports. And what is in the report of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in our view does not contradict this; for therein is only mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he stood for prayer, raised his hands extended - with no mention of the endpoint of that extension, to which place it is. It is possible it reaches parallel with the shoulders; and it is also possible that raising was before prayer for supplication, then he says takbir for prayer after that and raises his hands parallel with his shoulders. So the report of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, would be upon raising at standing for prayer for supplication, and the report of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, upon raising after that at prayer's opening - so these reports do not contradict. Others differed in that and said: he raises the hands at prayer's opening until parallel with the ears. And they argued for that with what:"
"1089","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said takbir for prayer's opening, he raised his hands until his thumbs were near the lobes of his ears. And with what:"
"1090","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when saying takbir for prayer, raise his hands level with his ears. And with what:"
"1091","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, and he mentioned with its isnad the same. And with what:"
"1092","Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus al-Susi al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Nasr ibn Asim, from Malik ibn al-Huwayrith, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: until parallel with them above his ears. And with what:"
"1093","Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Makhlad al-Asbahani related to me, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn Abi Hakim narrated to us, from Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Adawi, from al-Abbas ibn Sahl, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, that he used to say to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I am the most knowledgeable of you of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer: when he stood for prayer he said takbir and raised his hands level with his face. Abu Jafar said: So when these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed - wherein is the clarification of the raising to which place it is, at the point it ends - and the report of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with which we began, emerged as not contradicting them, we wished to examine which of these two meanings is preferable to hold."
"1094","Then Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wail ibn Hujr, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and saw him raise his hands level with his ears when saying takbir, when bowing, and when prostrating - he mentioned of this what Allah willed. He said: Then I came to him the next year, while they wore cloaks and hooded garments, and they raised their hands within them - and Sharik gestured to his chest. So Wail ibn Hujr informed in this report of his that their raising to their shoulders was only because their hands were then in their garments; and he informed that when their hands were bare they raised to level with their ears. So we acted upon his whole narration: we make the raising, when the hands are in garments due to cold, to the endpoint of what raising can reach - namely the shoulders; and when they are bare, he raised them to the ears, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. And it is not permissible to make the report of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and the like - wherein is mention of raising the hands to the shoulders - as being with hands bare; since it is possible they were in garments, so that would contradict what Wail ibn Hujr narrated, making the two reports contradictory. Rather we carry them upon agreement: we make the report of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing so with his hands in his garment, per what Wail related in his report. And we make what Wail narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, in other than cold conditions - raising his hands to his ears - recommended to hold and abandon its opposite. As for what we narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that - it is an error, and we shall clarify that in the chapter of raising the hands in bowing, Allah Most High willing. So by authenticating these reports is established what Wail narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), per what we detailed, of what he did in cold conditions and in other than cold conditions. And it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Most High have mercy on them."
"1095","Chapter 14: what is said in prayer after the opening takbir. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Zafar Abd al-Salam ibn Mutahhar narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Sulayman al-Dubai narrated to us, from Ali ibn Ali al-Rifai, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at night he said takbir then said: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name and exalted is Your grandeur; and there is no god besides You. Then he said: There is no god but Allah. Then he said: Allah is Most Great, greatly - thrice. Then he said: I seek refuge in Allah, the Hearing, the Knowing, from the accursed Satan: from his madness, his pride, and his poetry. Then he recited."
"1096","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its isnad, except that he did not say then he recited."
"1097","And Malik ibn Abd Allah ibn Sayf al-Tujibi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opened prayer, he raised his hands level with his shoulders, then said takbir, then said: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your grandeur, and there is no god besides You."
"1098","Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its isnad. And it has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, also that he used to say this also when opening prayer. As:"
"1099","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and said: Allahu Akbar; Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; blessed is Your name, exalted is Your grandeur. And as:"
"1100","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, and he mentioned with its isnad the same, adding: there is no god besides You. And as:"
"1101","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri related to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar, the same, except that he did not say at Dhu al-Hulayfah."
"1102","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, from Umar, the same, adding: making heard those next to him. And as:"
"1103","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the same. And as:"
"1104","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim related to me, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, that they heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say takbir, raising his voice, saying the same, so they would learn it. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted this and said: thus ought the praying person, when opening prayer, to say, adding nothing to this besides seeking refuge - if he is an imam or praying for himself; and among those who said that is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him."
"1105","And others differed with them and said: rather he should add after this what has been narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they mentioned what al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun narrated to us, from his uncle, from al-Araj, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he opened prayer, said: I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and the earth, upright, Muslim, and I am not of the polytheists. My prayer, my sacrifice, my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, with no partner for Him; and with that I have been commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims."
"1106","And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah al-Majishun informed us. And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi and Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, from al-Majishun and Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from al-Araj, and he mentioned with its isnad the same. And what:"
"1107","Al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from al-Araj, and he mentioned with its isnad the same. They said: So since narration came with this and with what preceded it, we preferred that the praying person say both together; and among those who said this is Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him."
"1108","Chapter 15: reciting Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in prayer. Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad informed us, he said: Khalid ibn Yazid informed me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Nuaym ibn al-Mujmir, he said: I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he recited: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful. Then when he reached: Not of those who earned anger nor of those astray, he said: Amin, so the people said Amin. Then he says when he gives salam: Indeed, by the One in whose hand is my soul, I am the most like you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1109","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Umm Salamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray in her house, reciting: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful. All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds. The Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful. Master of the Day of Judgment. You alone we worship and You alone we seek help. Guide us the straight path. The path of those You favored, not of those who earned anger nor of those astray. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted that Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim is part of the Fatihat al-Kitab, and that the praying person ought to recite it as he recites the Fatihat al-Kitab. And they also argued for that with what was narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as:"
"1110","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Dharr narrated to us, from his father, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza, from his father, he said: I prayed behind Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he recited aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim; and my father used to recite aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. And as:"
"1111","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Asim, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he recited it aloud. And as:"
"1112","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he never omitted Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim before the surah and after it, when he recited another surah in prayer. And as:"
"1113","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, he said: Yazid al-Faqir narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to open recitation with Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. And as:"
"1114","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, he said: I prayed behind Ibn al-Zubayr, and I heard him reciting Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim; Not of those who earned anger nor of those astray; Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. And they also argued for that with what:"
"1115","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from his father, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding: And We have given you seven of the oft-repeated - he said: the Fatihat al-Kitab. Then Ibn Abbas recited Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim and said: it is the seventh verse. He said: And he recited to Said ibn Jubayr as Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, had recited to him. And others differed with them and said: we do not hold reciting it aloud in prayer; then they differed after that. Some said: he says it silently; and some said: he does not say it at all, neither silently nor aloud. And they argued against the people of the first position with what:"
"1116","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn al-Qaqa narrated to us, he said: Abu Zuraah ibn Amr ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose in the second, he opened with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds, and did not pause. Abu Jafar said: So in this is proof that Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim is not part of the Fatihat al-Kitab; for had it been part of the Fatihat al-Kitab, he would have recited it in the second as he recited the Fatihat al-Kitab. And those who preferred reciting it aloud in the first rakah - because in their view it is part of the Fatihat al-Kitab - preferred that also in the second. So when it was negated by this report of Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it in the second, it was thereby also negated that he recited it in the first. So this report opposed the report of Nuaym ibn al-Mujmir, and this was preferable to it due to the uprightness of its chain and the superiority of its transmission's soundness over that of Nuaym's report. And they said: as for the report of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, which Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated - those who narrated it differed in its wording. Some narrated it as we mentioned, and others narrated it otherwise, as:"
"1117","Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Yala, that he asked Umm Salamah about the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recitation, so she described to him the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recitation, explained letter by letter. So in this, the mention of reciting Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim is from Umm Salamah describing thereby the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recitation of the rest of the Quran, how it was - and therein is no proof that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. So the meaning of this differs from the meaning of Ibn Jurayj's report. And it is also possible that the segmentation of the Fatihat al-Kitab in Ibn Jurayj's report was from Ibn Jurayj also, narrating thereby the explained recitation letter by letter which al-Layth narrated from Ibn Abi Mulaykah. So thereby it is negated that in the report of Umm Salamah is proof for anyone. And they also said to them, regarding what they narrated from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about His statement: And We have given you seven of the oft-repeated. As for what you mentioned that it is the seven oft-repeated - we do not dispute you in that. As for what you mentioned that Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim is of them - this has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, as you mentioned; and there has been narrated from others among those we narrated from in this chapter what indicates its opposite: that he did not recite it aloud. And they all did not differ that the Fatihat al-Kitab is seven verses: whoever made Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim of it counted it a verse, and whoever did not make it of it counted: You have favored them, a verse. So since they differed in that, examination became necessary - and we shall clarify that in its place, Allah Most High willing. And there has been narrated from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, what:"
"1118","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Awf, from Yazid al-Farisi, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I said to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him: What carried you to go to al-Anfal - it being of the seven long ones - and to Baraah - it being of the Mi'in - so you joined them, placed them among the seven long ones, and did not write between them the line Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. So Uthman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would receive the verse and say: Place it in the surah in which such-and-such is mentioned; and its story resembled its story. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died without my asking him about that, so I feared it was of it, so I joined them and did not write between them the line Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, and placed them among the seven long ones. Abu Jafar said: So here is Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, informing in this report that Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in his view was not of the surah, and that he only wrote it at the separation of surahs, it being other than them. So this opposes what Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, held of that. And reports have come successively transmitted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, that they did not recite it aloud in prayer."
"1119","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Qays ibn Abayah, he said: Ibn Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal related to me, from his father - and I have rarely seen a man more severe against innovation in Islam than him - so he heard me reciting Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, and said: O my son, beware of innovation in Islam; for I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, and I never heard it from any of them. But when you recite, say: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds. And as:"
"1120","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Said ibn Amir narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, used to open recitation with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds. And as:"
"1121","Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, and I never heard any of them recite aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. And as:"
"1122","Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik related to him, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I stood behind Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with them - all of them would not recite Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim when opening prayer. And as:"
"1123","Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that Abu Bakr and Umar - and Humayd thought he had mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the like. And as:"
"1124","Ahmad ibn Abi Imran and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shayban informed us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Anas saying: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, and I never heard any of them recite aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. And as:"
"1125","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Ahwas ibn Jawwab narrated to us, he said: Ammar ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shubah, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Neither the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor Abu Bakr, nor Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, used to recite aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. And as:"
"1126","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Imran al-Qasir, from al-Hasan, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim silently. And as:"
"1127","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Ubayd Allah al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Makhlad ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin and al-Hasan, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, used to open with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds. And as:"
"1128","Ahmad ibn Masud al-Khayyat al-Maqdisi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. And as:"
"1129","Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Muhammad ibn Lawh, brother of Banu Sad ibn Bakr, related to him, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, opening recitation with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"1130","Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad related to me, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Budayl, from Abu al-Jawza, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to open prayer with takbir, open recitation with: All praise is for Allah, and seal it with salam. Abu Jafar said: So when these reports became successively transmitted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, with what we mentioned - and in some of them that they opened recitation with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds - therein is no proof they did not mention Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim before it or after it; for by recitation here is only meant recitation of the Quran. So it is possible they did not count Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim as Quran, counting it a remembrance like: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, and what is said at prayer's opening. So what is recited of the Quran after that is opened with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds. And in some of them that they did not recite aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim - so therein is proof they said it without loud recitation; for otherwise their mentioning the negation of loudness would have no meaning. So by authenticating these reports is established abandoning loud recitation of Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim and mentioning it silently. And that has also been narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and others of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as:"
"1131","Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Sad, from Abu Wail, he said: Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, did not recite aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, nor the seeking of refuge, nor the amin."
"1132","Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: I heard Asim and Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding reciting aloud Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, he said: That is the practice of the bedouins."
"1133","And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, the same. Abu Jafar said: So this opposes what we narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the preceding section."
"1134","And as Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, that Sinan ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sadafi related to him, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, he said: I reached the imams, and they opened recitation only with: All praise is for Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"1135","Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, the same."
"1136","Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: I indeed reached men of our scholars who would not recite it."
"1137","And as Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, he said: I never heard al-Qasim reciting Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim. Abu Jafar said: So when it was established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and from those we mentioned after him, abandoning loud recitation of Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim, it was established that it is not of the Quran; for had it been of the Quran, it would have had to be recited aloud as other Quran besides it is recited aloud. Do you not see that the Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim in al-Naml is recited aloud, as other Quran besides it is recited aloud, because it is of the Quran. So when it was established that the one before the Fatihat al-Kitab is said silently while the Quran is recited aloud, it was established that it is not of the Quran; and it was established to say it silently and secretly, as the seeking of refuge, the opening, and the like are kept secret. And we have also seen it written at the openings of surahs in the mushaf - in the Fatihat al-Kitab and in others - and in other than the Fatihat al-Kitab it was not a verse, so it is likewise established that in the Fatihat al-Kitab it is not a verse. And this which was established - of negating Bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim being of the Fatihat al-Kitab, and of negating loud recitation of it in prayer - is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah Most High have mercy on them."
"1138","Chapter 16: recitation in Zuhr and Asr. Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Said and Hammad, the two sons of Zayd, narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam Musa ibn Salim, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: We were sitting among youths of Banu Hashim with Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, when a man said to him: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in Zuhr and Asr? He said: No. He said: Perhaps he used to recite to himself. In the report of Said, he said: No; and in the report of Hammad: it is worse than the first. Then he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a servant of Allah; Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded him and he conveyed - by Allah - what he was commanded."
"1139","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Yazid al-Madani relating from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that it was said to him: Some people recite in Zuhr and Asr, so he said: Had I power over them I would cut out their tongues; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited, so his recitation was a recitation for us and his silence was a silence for us. So some people adopted these reports we narrated, following them, and said: we do not hold that anyone recite in Zuhr and Asr at all. And they also narrated that from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, as:"
"1140","Abu Bishr Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah, from al-Walid ibn Qays, he said: I asked Suwayd ibn Ghafalah: Is there recitation in Zuhr and Asr? So he said: No. So it was said to them: you have no proof in what we narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both - for there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the opposite of that. As:"
"1141","Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I have memorized the Sunnah, except that I do not know whether the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Zuhr and Asr or not. So here is Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informing in this report that it was not verified with him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not recite in them; rather he commanded abandoning recitation in what our narration from him preceded, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not recite in that. So when it is negated that he had verified that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what he said of that is negated; for others have verified the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recitation in them, of what we shall mention in its place of this chapter, Allah Most High willing. Along with that there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, of his opinion what indicates the opposite of that, as:"
"1142","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Recite behind the imam the Fatihat al-Kitab in Zuhr and Asr."
"1143","Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, he said: I witnessed Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and I heard him saying: Do not pray a prayer except that you recite in it, even if the Fatihat al-Kitab."
"1144","And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi and Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Aliyah al-Barra, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him - or he was asked about recitation in Zuhr and Asr - so he said: He is your imam, so recite of it what is little and what is much; and nothing of the Quran is little. And as:"
"1145","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah informed us, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he mentioned the same. He said: And I asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: I am indeed shy to pray a prayer in which I do not recite the Mother of the Quran and what is easy. Abu Jafar said: So here is Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him - there has been narrated from him of his opinion that the follower recites behind the imam in Zuhr and Asr. And we have seen the imam bear for the follower, but we have not seen the follower bear anything for the imam. So when the follower recites, the imam is more deserving to recite - along with what we narrated from him also of commanding recitation in them. As for what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to what Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated of that:"
"1146","Then Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, that his father informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Zuhr and Asr, letting us hear the verse sometimes."
"1147","And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, from the Prophet, the like."
"1148","And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Muslim ibn Khalid, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to recite in the first two rakahs of Zuhr the Mother of the Quran and Quran, and in Asr the like of that; and in the last two of them the Mother of the Quran; and in Maghrib in the first two the Mother of the Quran and Quran, and in the third the Mother of the Quran. Ubayd Allah said: And I think he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1149","And Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah related to me, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite the Mother of the Quran and two surahs with it in the first two of Zuhr and Asr prayers, letting us hear the verse sometimes."
"1150","And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: Thirty of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered and said: Come, let us measure the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recitation in what he did not recite aloud of the prayers - and no two men of them differed. So they measured his recitation in the first two rakahs of Zuhr as the measure of thirty verses' recitation, and in the last two rakahs at half of that. And in Asr prayer in the first two rakahs at the measure of half of the first two in Zuhr, and in the last two rakahs at the measure of half of the last two rakahs of Zuhr."
"1151","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadhdhan, from al-Walid Abu Bishr ibn Muslim al-Anbari, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to stand in Zuhr in the first two rakahs, in each rakah the measure of thirty verses' recitation, and in the last two half of that; and he used to stand in Asr in the first two rakahs the measure of fifteen verses, and in the last two the measure of half of that."
"1152","And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi informed us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur ibn Zadhdhan narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We used to estimate the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing in Zuhr and Asr: we estimated his standing in Zuhr as thirty verses, the measure of Surah al-Sajdah, in the first two rakahs, and in the last two at half of that; and we estimated his standing in the first two rakahs of Asr at the measure of the last two of Zuhr, and we estimated his standing in the last two rakahs of Asr at half of that."
"1153","And Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak, from Jabir ibn Samurah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Zuhr and Asr: By the heaven and the night-comer, By the heaven full of stars, and the like of surahs."
"1154","And Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: A man recited behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Zuhr and Asr; then when he finished he said: Which of you recited: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High? A man said: I did. He said: I knew that some of you contended with me for it."
"1155","And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, that Zurarah related to them, from Imran, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1156","And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah, from Imran, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"1157","And Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed us, from Abu Makhlad, from Ibn Umar, he said: And I did not hear it from him - that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in Zuhr prayer. He said: So his Companions thought he had recited: Tanzil, al-Sajdah."
"1158","And Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla informed us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lead us, reciting aloud and silently; so we recited aloud in what he recited aloud, and silently in what he kept silent. And I heard him saying: There is no prayer except with recitation."
"1159","And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Raqabah, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: In every prayer there is recitation: what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) let us hear, we let you hear; and what he concealed from us, we concealed from you."
"1160","And Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Habib al-Muallim, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the same."
"1161","1237 - And Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ata, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say, then he mentioned the like of it."
"1162","1238 - And Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Habib al-Mu'allim informed us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), the like of it."
"1163","1239 - And Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"1164","1240 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, he said: Abu Ubaydah - and he is Humayd al-Tawil - informed me, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in Zuhr: (Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High). Abu Ja'far said: And a group also argued concerning that, along with what we mentioned, by what was reported from Khabbab ibn al-Aratt. As:"
"1165","1241 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Ma'mar, he said: We said to Khabbab: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in Zuhr and Asr? He said: Yes. I said: By what did you recognize that? He said: By the movement of his beard. And as:"
"1166","1242 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik, Abu Mu'awiyah and Waki' informed us, from al-A'mash, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In this there is no proof, in our view, that he used to recite in them, for his beard may have moved due to a tasbih he uttered, or a supplication, or other than that. But what established his recitation in these two prayers is from those we have reported the reports from in the section before this. When it was established by what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he did recite in Zuhr and Asr, and what was reported from Ibn Abbas that contradicts that was negated, we returned to examining after that whether we find therein what indicates the correctness of one of the two views we mentioned. We considered that and saw that standing in prayer is obligatory, and likewise bowing, and likewise prostration, and all of this is from the obligation of prayer, and prayer contains it such that prayer does not suffice if anything of that is omitted, and that is the same in all prayers. And we saw the first sitting is a Sunnah, with no disagreement therein, and it is the same in all prayers. And we saw the last sitting is disputed among people: some say it is obligatory and some say it is a Sunnah, and each group made that the same in all prayers. So these things, whatever of them is obligatory in one prayer is obligatory in all prayers. And audible recitation in night prayer is not obligatory but a Sunnah, and prayer is not bound by it as it is bound by bowing, prostration and standing. So that may be absent in some prayers and established in others. But what is obligatory, with which prayer is bound and does not suffice without attaining it, when it is obligatory in some prayers it is likewise in the rest. When we saw recitation in Maghrib, Isha and Fajr is necessary in the view of this opponent, unavoidable, and prayer does not suffice without it, it is likewise in Zuhr and Asr. This is a decisive proof against whoever denies recitation in Zuhr and Asr among those who deem it obligatory in others. As for whoever does not deem recitation part of the essence of prayer, the proof against him is that we have seen Maghrib and Isha recited in both in his view, aloud in the first two rak'ahs of them and silently in the rest. When the Sunnah of what is after the first two rak'ahs was recitation, and it did not fall by the absence of audibility, the analogy is that the Sunnah in Zuhr and Asr is likewise: the absence of audibility does not remove recitation, by analogy with what we mentioned. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And that was reported from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1167","1243 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad and Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: I heard from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting in Zuhr and Asr: (Qaf, by the Glorious Qur'an)."
"1168","1244 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri narrating from Ibn Abi Rafi', from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to command, or liked, to recite behind the imam in Zuhr and Asr, in the first two rak'ahs the Opening of the Book and a surah, and in the last two the Opening of the Book."
"1169","1245 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, he said: I heard Abu Maryam al-Asadi say: I heard Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting in Zuhr."
"1170","1246 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Murrah and Hakim, that they entered upon Muwarriq al-Ijli and he led them in Zuhr and recited Qaf and al-Dhariyat, letting them hear some of his recitation. When he finished he said: I prayed behind Ibn Umar and he recited Qaf and al-Dhariyat, and let us hear the like of what I let you hear."
"1171","1247 - And Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Haywah and Ibn Lahi'ah, they said: Bakr ibn Amr informed us that Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam informed him that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said to him: When you pray alone, recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr in each the Mother of the Qur'an and a surah, and in the last two rak'ahs the Mother of the Qur'an. He said: Then I met Zayd ibn Thabit and Jabir ibn Abdullah, and they said the like of what Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said."
"1172","1248 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdullah about recitation in Zuhr and Asr, and he said: As for me, I recite in the first two the Opening of the Book and a surah each, and in the last two the Opening of the Book."
"1173","1249 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that he asked him: What do you do in your prayers in which you do not recite aloud when you are in your houses? He said: We recite in the first two of Zuhr and Asr in each rak'ah the Opening of the Book and a surah, and we recite in the last two the Mother of the Qur'an, and we supplicate."
"1174","1250 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Makhramah informed me, from his father, from Ubaydullah ibn Miqsam, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: When you pray alone any of the prayers, recite in the first two rak'ahs a surah along with the Mother of the Qur'an, and in the last two the Mother of the Qur'an."
"1175","1251 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, he said: Yazid al-Faqir narrated to me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, I heard him say: He recites in the first two rak'ahs the Opening of the Book and a surah, and in the last two the Opening of the Book. He said: And we used to say that there is no prayer except with recitation of the Opening of the Book and what is above that, or what is more than that."
"1176","1252 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Zakariyya, from Abdullah ibn Khabbab, from Khalid ibn Urfutah, he said: I heard Khabbab reciting in Zuhr and Asr: (When shaken)."
"1177","1253 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, he said: I heard Hisham ibn Isma'il at the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Abu al-Darda said: Recite in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and Asr the Opening of the Book and two surahs, and in the last two the Opening of the Book."
"1178","17 - Chapter of recitation in the Maghrib prayer. 1254 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father. (H)."
"1179","1255 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri informed me, from Ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in Maghrib: al-Tur."
"1180","1256 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Malik and Sufyan informed us, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1181","1257 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, he said: One of my brothers narrated to me, from his father, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Badr. He said: I reached him while he was praying Maghrib, and he recited al-Tur, and it was as if my heart split when I heard the Qur'an, and that was before he embraced Islam."
"1182","1258 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), that he said: Umm al-Fadl bint al-Harith heard him reciting: (By those sent forth in succession). She said: O my son, your recitation of this surah reminded me that it was the last thing I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the Maghrib prayer."
"1183","1259 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"1184","1260 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed us that he heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr say: Zayd ibn Thabit informed me that he said to Marwan ibn al-Hakam: O Abu Abd al-Malik, what makes you recite in the Maghrib prayer (Say: He is Allah, One) and another small surah. Zayd said: By Allah, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in the Maghrib prayer the longest of the long ones, and it is Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad."
"1185","1261 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"1186","1262 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, that Marwan used to recite in Maghrib Surah Ya-Sin. Urwah said: Zayd ibn Thabit - or Abu Zayd al-Ansari, Hisham doubted - said to Marwan: Why do you shorten the Maghrib prayer, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite therein the longest of the two long ones: al-A'raf."
"1187","1263 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdul-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, from Umm al-Fadl bint al-Harith, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Maghrib in his house, in a single garment wrapped around him, and he recited (By those sent forth). He did not pray any prayer after it until he passed away. So a group claimed they take these reports and follow them. Others opposed them in their view and said: One should not recite in Maghrib except the short Mufassal. They said: It is possible that his saying he recited al-Tur means he recited part of it, and that is permissible in the language; it is said: So-and-so recites the Qur'an when he recites something of it. And his reciting al-Tur may mean he recited all of it. So we looked whether anything was reported indicating one of the two interpretations."
"1188","1264 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from his father, he said: I came to Madinah in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to speak to him about the captives of Badr. I reached him while he was praying Maghrib with his Companions, and I heard him reciting: (Indeed, the punishment of your Lord will occur). It was as if my heart split. When he finished I spoke to him about them, and a shaykh said: Had he come to me I would have interceded for him - meaning his father Mut'im ibn Adi. So this Hushaym reported this hadith from al-Zuhri and clarified the story as it was, and informed that what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: (Indeed, the punishment of your Lord will occur). This shows that his saying in the first hadith, he recited al-Tur, means only what he heard him recite of it. And the wording of Jubayr is only what Hushaym reported, for he related the story as it was. So what is told therein from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his recitation of: (Indeed, the punishment of your Lord will occur) specifically. As for the hadith of Malik, it is an abridgment of this, and likewise the saying of Zayd ibn Thabit in his saying to Marwan: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting therein the longest of the long ones, Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad - it may mean his reciting part of it. And among what also indicates the correctness of this interpretation:"
"1189","1265 - That Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, that they used to pray Maghrib then shoot arrows."
"1190","1266 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad and Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, they said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Thabit informed us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We used to pray Maghrib with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then one of us would shoot and see the spot of his arrow."
"1191","1267 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"1192","1268 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr. (H). 1269 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Awanah and Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Ali ibn Bilal, he said: I prayed with a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the Ansar, and they told me they used to pray Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then go out shooting so that the landing of their arrows was not hidden from them, until they reached their homes, while they were in the farthest part of Madinah, among Banu Salimah."
"1193","1270 - Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Khayyat narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from one of Banu Salimah, that they used to pray Maghrib with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then return to their families while seeing the spot of the arrow at a distance of two-thirds of a mile."
"1194","1271 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: We used to pray Maghrib with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then come to Banu Salimah while we could see the spots of the arrows. Since this was the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finishing the Maghrib prayer, it is impossible that he had recited therein al-A'raf, or even half of it."
"1195","1272 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: Mu'adh led his companions in Maghrib and opened with Surah al-Baqarah or al-Nisa. A man prayed then left. That reached Mu'adh and he said: He is a hypocrite. That reached the man, so he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you a source of trial, O Mu'adh? He said it twice. Had you recited: (Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High) - (By the sun and its brightness), for behind you pray the one with a need, the weak, the small and the old."
"1196","1273 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"1197","1274 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir, he said: It is the darkness (al-Atamah)."
"1198","1275 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal used to pray with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then return and lead us. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed Isha one night, so Mu'adh ibn Jabal prayed with him, then came to lead us and opened with Surah al-Baqarah. When a man of the people saw that, he stepped aside and prayed alone. We said: What is wrong with you, O so-and-so, have you turned hypocrite? He said: I have not turned hypocrite, and I shall surely come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Mu'adh prays with you then returns and leads us, and you delayed Isha last night, so he prayed with you, then came and stepped forward to lead us and opened with Surah al-Baqarah. When I saw that I stepped aside and prayed alone. O Messenger of Allah, we are owners of water-camels; we work with our limbs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you a source of trial, O Mu'adh - twice - recite such a surah, recite such a surah, short surahs of the Mufassal, I do not recall which. We said to Amr: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Recite Surah (By the night when it covers), (By the sun and its brightness), (By the sky full of stars), (By the sky and the night-comer). Amr ibn Dinar said: And it is like this. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thus disapproved of Mu'adh making his recitation heavy upon them with Surah al-Baqarah, and said to him: Are you a source of trial, O Mu'adh? And he commanded him with the surahs of the Mufassal we mentioned. If that prayer was the Maghrib prayer, this hadith contradicts the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit and what we mentioned with it at the beginning of this chapter. And if it was the last Isha prayer, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked reciting therein what we mentioned despite the spaciousness of its time, then the Maghrib prayer - with the narrowness of its time - is more deserving that such recitation therein be disliked. And there was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what he used to recite in the last Isha prayer something like this."
"1199","1276 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mu'min al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the last Isha prayer: (By the sun and its brightness) and the like of surahs. If one says: Was it reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he recited in Maghrib the short Mufassal? It is said to him: Yes."
"1200","1277 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), from Amir, from Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in Maghrib: By the fig and the olive."
